# TRANSPORT IN PLANTS

e('3.7-01','Root structure',[
'A young dicotyledonous root has an outer epidermis, some of whose cells extend into root hairs, surrounding a broad cortex.',
'In a typical young dicotyledonous root cross-section, xylem is central and star-shaped, with phloem between its arms.'
],'''A root hair is an extension of one epidermal cell, not a separate root. Its thin wall and large surface area bring the cell close to soil water. The cortex consists of many cells between the epidermis and the transport tissues. Water crosses this region on its way to the xylem. Xylem transports water and mineral ions; phloem carries sucrose and amino acids.

Start with the whole organ before identifying individual cells. In a transverse section, the epidermis forms the outside boundary and the cortex is the wide band inside it. At the centre, the xylem forms a star shape made of large, thick-walled cells, and the smaller cells in the gaps between the arms of the star are phloem. Root hairs grow only near the growing tip, so a section cut further back may show none; the central xylem star still identifies the organ as a root.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','30.3 Roots','paraphrase'))
c('3.7-01','FEATURE','Fig. VIS-6116-3.7-01 is a photomicrograph of a transverse section of a young dicotyledonous root. Identify the star-shaped tissue at the centre and the tissue lying between the arms of the star.','The star-shaped tissue at the centre is xylem. The tissue between its arms is phloem.','1 mark: names the star-shaped central tissue as xylem. 1 mark: names the tissue between the arms of the star as phloem. Each mark is for one correctly named structure in the correct position; the two names must not be swapped.',True)
e('3.7-02','Recognising a dicotyledonous stem',[
'A young dicotyledonous stem has an epidermis surrounding a cortex, with vascular bundles arranged in a ring.',
'Within a typical young dicotyledonous stem vascular bundle, xylem faces the centre and phloem faces the outside.'
],'''The epidermis protects the surface. The cortex lies beneath it. Each vascular bundle contains transport tissues, but their arrangement differs from that in the young root: the bundles form a ring near the edge of the stem instead of a central star. In a stem cross-section, follow the circle of bundles and pick out the inner xylem and the outer phloem in one bundle before identifying them elsewhere. Tissue position is more reliable than colour, because different stains colour tissues differently.

A longitudinal section shows vessels running along the stem rather than a ring of bundles. Section direction therefore changes the pattern seen, without changing the function of the tissue. To tell a root from a stem in an unlabelled transverse section, look at the arrangement: a central star of xylem means root, a ring of separate bundles means stem.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','30.2 Stems','paraphrase'))
c('3.7-02','FEATURE','Fig. VIS-6116-3.7-02 is a photomicrograph of a transverse section of a young dicotyledonous stem showing one vascular bundle. Identify the transport tissue on the side of the bundle nearer the centre of the stem and the transport tissue on the side nearer the outside.','Nearer the centre: xylem. Nearer the outside: phloem.','1 mark: names the tissue nearer the centre of the stem as xylem. 1 mark: names the tissue nearer the outside of the bundle as phloem. Each mark is for one correctly named structure, judged by its position relative to the centre of the stem.',True)
e('3.7-03','Drawing root and stem sections',[
'A low-power biological plan drawing records the relative sizes and positions of tissues visible in a specimen, without adding invented cells.',
'A detailed drawing of transport tissue should show observed cell outlines and label recognisable xylem vessels and phloem accurately.'
],'''Place a prepared slide on the microscope stage and locate the section at low power. Adjust focus and illumination before drawing; then use higher power for a small region if individual transport cells are needed. Use a large drawing with single clear lines, no decorative shading and labels ruled to the exact structure. Keep relative proportions faithful to the section. State what was drawn and the section direction. Draw only what you can see in the specimen, not structures remembered from a textbook diagram.

Xylem vessels have conspicuous thick walls and open spaces inside. Phloem is identified from its position and the cells you can observe, not drawn as an invented large empty tube. An outline of the outside of a root cannot replace a drawing of a tissue section. The microscope procedure builds on the skills in 1.2.4.''',src=None)
c('3.7-03','APP','Fig. VIS-6116-3.7-03 is a photomicrograph of a transverse section of a young dicotyledonous root, with a box around part of the xylem and phloem. Draw a large labelled plan of the whole section and a detailed drawing of the boxed region. Label the epidermis, cortex, xylem and phloem.','A large plan with an outer epidermis, a broad cortex and central transport tissues in their true proportions, drawn in single unshaded lines and labelled. A separate enlarged drawing of the boxed cells showing xylem vessels and phloem, with labels.','1 mark: the plan is large and shows the tissues in their correct relative proportions. 1 mark: lines are single, clear and unshaded, and no individual cells are drawn in the plan. 1 mark: the epidermis, cortex, xylem and phloem are all labelled correctly, with label lines touching the tissues. 1 mark: the detailed drawing of the boxed region shows xylem vessels and phloem cells as they appear in this photomicrograph, so features that cannot be seen are not added.',True)
c('3.7-03','WHY','Explain why a learner should first examine an entire root cross-section at low power before making a detailed drawing of its transport tissues.','Low power shows the arrangement of the whole section. This lets the learner locate and select the correct tissue region for the detailed view.','1 mark: gives the reason that low power shows the whole section and how its tissues are arranged. 1 mark: gives the purpose, that this lets the learner choose the correct region, seen in its place among the other tissues, for the detailed drawing.')
e('3.7-04','Water from soil to atmosphere',[
'Water enters a root hair cell by osmosis when soil water has a higher water potential than the cell contents, crossing a partially permeable membrane.',
'Water crosses the root through cell walls and through cells, enters xylem, and moves upwards in a continuous column pulled by evaporation from leaves.',
'In a leaf, water moves from xylem to mesophyll tissues, evaporates from moist cell surfaces, and diffuses as vapour through stomata.'
],'''Osmosis describes the parts of the route where water crosses a partially permeable membrane; it does not describe bulk flow along an open xylem vessel. Water moving through root cell walls need not cross a membrane at each successive wall. The route includes membrane crossings before the water enters the xylem. The cortex is a band of many cells, not a single membrane, and root cells do not pump water up the stem.

Evaporation from mesophyll surfaces removes liquid water from the leaf. Replacement water is drawn from the xylem, producing a pull transmitted down the connected water column. Attraction between water molecules helps the column stay continuous. Xylem vessel walls are strengthened so they resist collapse under this pull.

Water supply depends on the soil-to-root gradient and an intact conducting route. A plant can therefore wilt in wet soil if root uptake or xylem transport is damaged. Mineral-ion uptake is a separate process from water movement, because ions are often taken up by active transport (see 3.3.3).''')
c('3.7-04','PROC','Explain water entry into a root hair when soil water has a higher water potential than the cell contents.','Water enters by osmosis. It moves across the partially permeable cell membrane, down the water potential gradient from the soil water into the cell, so the cell gains water.','1 mark: names the process as osmosis. 1 mark: states that water then moves across the partially permeable cell membrane, down the water potential gradient, so the cell gains water.')
c('3.7-04','MECH','Explain the complete route and mechanism of water movement from moist soil through a plant to the air. Assume soil water has a higher water potential than root hair contents.','Water enters root hairs by osmosis. It crosses the cortex, through cell walls and cells, into the xylem. Water evaporates from the moist surfaces of mesophyll cells, and this pulls a continuous column of water up the xylem. Water vapour diffuses out through the stomata into the air.','1 mark: water enters the root hairs by osmosis. 1 mark: it then crosses the cortex, through cell walls and cells, into the xylem. 1 mark: evaporation from the surfaces of mesophyll cells pulls a continuous column of water up the xylem. 1 mark: water vapour diffuses out through the stomata, so water is lost to the air.')
c('3.7-04','APP','Explain why a shoot supplied with water still wilts after a narrow section of its xylem is crushed, although its phloem remains intact.','Crushed xylem restricts water flow to the leaves. Transpiration then exceeds replacement, so leaf cells lose turgor.','1 mark: the crushed xylem cannot carry water up to the leaves (phloem does not transport water). 1 mark: because water is still lost by transpiration but not replaced, leaf cells lose turgor, so this shoot wilts.',demand=inference('Water supplied; selective xylem damage with phloem intact.','Identify failure of transport rather than soil shortage and infer water deficit in leaves.'))
c('3.7-04','MISCON','A learner says that one transport tissue carries water up from the roots to the leaves and also carries sucrose from the leaves to a growing bud. Explain the error and name the tissue that carries each substance.','Two different tissues are involved. Xylem carries water and mineral ions up from the roots. Phloem carries sucrose and amino acids from the leaves to the bud and other regions of use or storage.','1 mark: names xylem as the tissue that carries water and mineral ions up from the roots. 1 mark: explains the error, that sucrose travels to the bud in phloem instead, a separate tissue from the xylem.')
e('3.7-05','Tracing the water pathway with dye',[
'A cut leafy shoot placed in a suitable aqueous dye can show the water-conducting pathway when coloured tissue is examined in stem and leaf sections.',
'Dye location indicates where the solution travelled under the investigation conditions, but does not by itself measure water-transport speed.'
],'''Choose fresh similar shoots and use a suitable dilute stain selected by the teacher. Cut the stem under water to reduce the entry of air into the conducting tubes, then place it promptly in the stain. A similar shoot in water without dye shows what natural colour looks like. Keep exposure time, shoot size and environment comparable. Examine fresh transverse sections at measured heights and inspect leaf veins after a fixed interval. Handle the cutting tool using the laboratory's supervised procedure.

Record which tissues are coloured and repeat with several shoots. Coloured xylem, rather than an evenly coloured stem surface, supports a transport-pathway conclusion. Rinse the outside before sectioning to reduce contamination. If no colour appears, the likely causes are too short a time, air in the xylem or an unsuitable stain; it does not show that the plant has no xylem.''',src=None)
c('3.7-05','SYNTH','Describe an investigation using a suitable stain to find the water pathway in a leafy shoot.','Change the supplied liquid: teacher-selected dilute stain or plain water. Measure the location and height of internal colour after a fixed time. Use similar shoots, liquid volumes and environments. Cut shoots under water, stand them in the liquids and examine safe teacher-cut stem sections and leaf veins. Repeat with several shoots in each treatment.','1 mark: the independent variable is changed: dilute stain compared with plain water. 1 mark: the dependent variable is measured: which tissues are coloured, and how high the colour has reached, after a fixed time. 1 mark: at least two variables are kept the same, such as similar shoots, volume of liquid, time and temperature. 1 mark: the stems are cut under water before being stood in the liquids, so air does not block the xylem. 1 mark: several shoots are used for each liquid, so the results are repeated and reliable.')
c('3.7-05','LIM','Suggest a limitation of cutting a stained stem without cleaning its outside, and an improvement.','Surface dye can smear onto tissues and falsely suggest internal transport. Rinse and blot the outside before cutting a fresh section with a clean blade.','1 mark: the limitation is that dye on the outside of the stem can be smeared onto the cut surface and mistaken for dye that travelled up inside. 1 mark: an improvement, such as rinsing and blotting the outside before cutting, or cutting a fresh section with a clean blade.')
c('3.7-05','APP','Suggest what can be concluded when internal xylem is coloured in three dye-fed shoots but not in three plain-water shoots, and give one limit to that conclusion.','The stained solution travelled through xylem in this investigation. The colour alone does not establish its transport rate.','1 mark: concludes that the dye solution travelled up through the xylem, because this tissue was coloured only in the dye-fed shoots. 1 mark: one valid limit, such as that the colour shows only the route and not the speed of transport, or that three shoots per treatment is a small sample.')
e('3.7-06','The meaning of transpiration',[
'Transpiration is loss of water vapour from plant leaves by evaporation of water at the surfaces of mesophyll cells followed by diffusion of water vapour through the stomata.',
'The site of evaporation is the moist mesophyll surface, whereas a stoma is a route by which the vapour leaves the leaf.'
],'''The definition contains two physical processes in order. Evaporation changes liquid water to vapour inside the leaf. Diffusion then moves vapour from the air spaces towards the outside air when the vapour concentration is lower outside. Water does not first become vapour inside the stoma. A stoma is a pore, not a water-producing cell. Root absorption supplies replacement water but is not itself transpiration.''',src=('SRC-SYL-6116','3.7 Transport in plants: definition of transpiration','direct'))
c('3.7-06','DEF','Define transpiration.','Loss of water vapour from plant leaves by evaporation of water at the surfaces of mesophyll cells followed by diffusion of water vapour through the stomata.','1 mark: water evaporates from the surfaces of the mesophyll cells. 1 mark: the water vapour then diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata. The definition must include both processes and refer to loss of water vapour from the leaves.')
c('3.7-06','MISCON','Explain the mistake in saying water evaporates at the stomatal pore. State where vapour forms and how it leaves.','Water evaporates at moist mesophyll cell surfaces. The resulting vapour diffuses through the stomata.','1 mark: water evaporates from the moist surfaces of mesophyll cells into the air spaces, so the error is placing evaporation at the pore. 1 mark: the vapour then leaves the leaf by diffusion through the stomata.')
e('3.7-07','Describing the transpiration stream',[
'Transpiration draws replacement water from leaf xylem as mesophyll surfaces lose water by evaporation.',
'The stream through xylem supplies water for cells and transports dissolved mineral ions from roots.'
],'''Trace liquid water upwards through root, stem and leaf veins, then distinguish liquid from vapour at the mesophyll. The water reaching the leaf can support cell turgor or supply reactions such as photosynthesis as well as being lost as vapour. The stream is a flow through connected vessels, not the movement of a series of separate water-containing cells. Transpiration continues according to environmental conditions and stomatal opening, whether or not the leaf is actively photosynthesising at that moment.''')
c('3.7-07','PROC','Describe the last two steps by which water leaves a leaf during transpiration.','First, liquid water evaporates from moist mesophyll cell surfaces into leaf air spaces. Then water vapour diffuses out through stomata.','1 mark: liquid water evaporates from the moist surfaces of mesophyll cells into the air spaces. 1 mark: then the water vapour diffuses out through the stomata, so it reaches the outside air.')
# Environmental factors: explicit differences, controlled conditions, consequential cases.
for oid,heading,claims,explanation in [
('3.7-08','Temperature and water loss',['With adequate water supply and similar stomatal opening, a higher temperature increases evaporation from mesophyll surfaces.','Faster vapour diffusion and evaporation can increase transpiration and replacement water uptake when other conditions are comparable.'],'''A temperature comparison must keep light, humidity and air movement as similar as possible. Heating with a lamp may change temperature and light together, so it would not isolate one factor. Higher temperature increases transpiration as long as the stomata stay open and water is available. Severe heat or water shortage may close the stomata and reverse the expected increase. A greater uptake reading supports increased water demand, but some absorbed water is retained in growth and cell contents.

Worked example: a shoot takes up 4.0 cm³ of water in 20 min at 20 °C and 7.0 cm³ in 20 min at 30 °C. The rates are 0.20 and 0.35 cm³/min, so uptake is 0.15 cm³/min greater at the higher temperature.'''),
('3.7-09','Wind and the boundary layer',['Air movement removes humid air from near a leaf surface.','Where stomata remain open and water supply is adequate, removal of humid air steepens the vapour concentration gradient and tends to increase transpiration.'],'''In still air, vapour leaving the stomata accumulates close to the leaf. A gentle air current replaces this with drier air. The effect is on the air next to the stomata; wind does not push liquid water out of the leaf. Strong drying may cause the stomata to close, so uptake does not keep rising with wind speed. Keep the fan far enough away to avoid heating or physically damaging the shoot and measure over a fixed time.'''),
('3.7-10','Humidity and the vapour gradient',['Humidity describes the water vapour in air; more humid air reduces the vapour concentration difference between leaf air spaces and the atmosphere.','With other conditions held similar, higher humidity tends to reduce transpiration and hence replacement water uptake.'],'''The mechanism is diffusion, not absorption of air into roots. Saturated air outside a leaf leaves little gradient for net outward diffusion, even if liquid water can still evaporate and condense locally. Humidity and temperature must be considered separately: changing temperature can change relative humidity as well as evaporation. To test humidity, keep air temperature and movement matched and let the shoot adjust to the new conditions before timing uptake.'''),
('3.7-11','Light and stomatal opening',['In many plants, illumination promotes stomatal opening, allowing water vapour to diffuse out of the leaf.','If the plant is adequately watered and temperature, humidity and wind are comparable, increased light can increase transpiration and water uptake.'],'''Light does not supply a pushing force inside xylem. Its effect is commonly mediated by opening stomata for gas exchange. Other causes of closure, including water stress, can limit the effect. A lamp also heats leaves; a heat screen, distance adjustment and temperature measurement help separate the effects. Some desert succulents open their stomata mainly at night, so their water loss is greatest then.''')]: e(oid,heading,claims,explanation)
c('3.7-08','CHAIN','Explain why a moderate temperature increase can increase water uptake in a well-watered leafy shoot with stomata remaining open.','Higher temperature speeds evaporation from mesophyll surfaces. The increased transpiration draws more replacement water through xylem.','1 mark: when the temperature increases, water evaporates faster from the mesophyll surfaces. 1 mark: so the rate of transpiration increases and more replacement water is pulled up the xylem, which increases uptake.')
c('3.7-08','APP','Explain why a shoot at 36 °C takes up less water than at 26 °C when measurements show that its stomata have closed at 36 °C.','Closure reduces the route for outward vapour diffusion. This can outweigh faster evaporation at the higher temperature, reducing transpiration pull and uptake.','1 mark: the closed stomata block the route by which water vapour diffuses out of the leaf. 1 mark: this outweighs the faster evaporation at 36 °C, so transpiration falls and less water is pulled up the xylem.',demand=inference('Hotter shoot has independently observed stomatal closure and lower uptake.','Resolve a reversed temperature trend by integrating aperture evidence.'))
c('3.7-09','CHAIN','Explain how a gentle breeze increases transpiration from a well-watered leaf while stomata remain open.','The breeze removes humid air close to the leaf. A steeper vapour concentration gradient increases outward diffusion.','1 mark: when a breeze blows, the humid air next to the stomata is replaced by drier air. 1 mark: so the vapour concentration gradient is steeper, vapour diffuses out faster and the rate of transpiration increases.')
c('3.7-09','APP','Explain why a fan increases water uptake when it blows across leaves, but has little effect when the same fan is redirected across the sealed water reservoir. Leaf temperature remains unchanged.','Airflow at leaves removes humid air and increases vapour diffusion. Airflow at the sealed reservoir does not change the gradient beside the stomata.','1 mark: air blown across the leaves removes humid air from beside the stomata, steepening the vapour gradient, so transpiration and uptake increase. 1 mark: this sealed reservoir loses no water vapour, so air moving over it does not change transpiration and uptake stays the same.',demand=inference('Same fan placed at leaf or sealed reservoir, unchanged temperature.','Localise the causal effect to the leaf surface.'))
c('3.7-10','CHAIN','Explain how raising humidity affects transpiration when leaf temperature and stomatal opening are unchanged.','More humid external air reduces the water-vapour concentration gradient. Less vapour diffuses out, so transpiration falls.','1 mark: when humidity rises, the air outside holds more water vapour, so the concentration gradient from the leaf to the air is smaller. 1 mark: less vapour diffuses out, so the rate of transpiration falls.')
c('3.7-10','APP','Explain why an enclosed transparent bag containing moist cotton reduces a shoot’s water uptake without changing its light level or temperature. The cotton does not touch the plant.','Evaporation from the cotton raises humidity around leaves. This reduces outward vapour diffusion and the demand for replacement water.','1 mark: water evaporating from the moist cotton raises the humidity of the air around the leaves. 1 mark: this makes the vapour gradient smaller, so less vapour diffuses out and less replacement water is drawn up.',demand=inference('Wet cotton separated from shoot; light and temperature matched.','Infer an air-humidity mechanism rather than root watering.'))
c('3.7-11','CHAIN','Explain why light can increase transpiration in an adequately watered plant whose stomata open in daylight. Assume leaf temperature is unchanged.','Light promotes stomatal opening. More vapour can diffuse through the open pores, increasing transpiration.','1 mark: when light intensity increases, the stomata open, or open wider. 1 mark: more water vapour diffuses out through the pores, so the rate of transpiration increases.')
c('3.7-11','APP','Explain why increasing lamp brightness fails to increase water uptake when a transparent coating has sealed the stomata. Leaf temperature is kept constant.','The coating prevents additional vapour diffusion through stomata. Extra light cannot restore that exit route, so it need not increase transpiration pull.','1 mark: the coating seals the stomata, blocking the route by which water vapour leaves the leaf. 1 mark: so extra light cannot increase transpiration, because vapour still cannot escape while this route is blocked.',demand=inference('Illuminated leaves with sealed stomata and constant temperature.','Infer why the usual light relationship fails when its route is blocked.'))
e('3.7-12','Counting stomata with impressions',[
'A clear varnish impression can reproduce stomatal outlines from a leaf surface for counting under a light microscope.',
'Upper and lower epidermis must be compared over equal areas with the same magnification and representative repeated fields.'
],'''Use a thin layer of teacher-approved clear water-based varnish on a marked area of each side of a fresh leaf. Let it dry, lift the impression with transparent tape and mount it flat on a labelled slide. Focus on the impression rather than pressing a leaf into the microscope. Count stomata in several non-overlapping fields on each surface. Keep magnification, field area and the counting rule at field boundaries constant. Average the counts separately. Use several leaves of the same species and similar age, preferably paired surfaces of each leaf.

For example, equal-area fields with lower-surface counts 14, 16 and 15 give a mean of 15; upper-surface counts 2, 1 and 3 give a mean of 2. If each field has an area of 0.20 mm², the lower surface has 15 ÷ 0.20 = 75 stomata per mm² and the upper surface 2 ÷ 0.20 = 10 per mm². This sample supports more stomata below, but other plants differ: many floating leaves have stomata only on the upper surface. Distorted impressions or views crossing a large vein may bias the comparison.''',src=None)
c('3.7-12','SYNTH','Describe an investigation comparing stomatal numbers on upper and lower surfaces of the same type of leaf using clear varnish.','Change the leaf surface sampled. Measure stomata per equal microscope field. Make dry varnish impressions, lift with tape and mount flat. Keep species, leaf age, field area and magnification the same. Count several random non-overlapping fields on each surface of several leaves and compare means.','1 mark: the independent variable is changed: upper surface compared with lower surface. 1 mark: the dependent variable is measured: the number of stomata in each equal-area field. 1 mark: at least two variables are kept the same, such as species, leaf age, field area and magnification. 1 mark: the varnish is left to dry, then lifted with clear tape and mounted flat on a slide. 1 mark: several fields on several leaves are counted and a mean calculated for each surface, so the comparison is reliable.')
c('3.7-12','LIM','Suggest a limitation of comparing a single field from each leaf surface, and an improvement.','Stomata may be unevenly distributed, so one field may be unrepresentative. Count several randomly chosen equal-area fields and compare mean counts.','1 mark: the limitation is that stomata are not spread evenly, so one field may not represent the whole surface. 1 mark: an improvement is to count several randomly chosen equal-area fields on each surface and compare the means.')
c('3.7-12','APP','Fig. VIS-6116-3.7-12 shows varnish impressions of the upper and lower epidermis of one leaf, viewed at the same magnification over equal areas. Suggest which surface has more stomata per unit area and give the evidence.','The lower surface. In most dicotyledonous leaves the lower epidermis has more stomata, so its impression shows the larger count in the same area. Count the stomata in each equal-area view and compare the counts directly, because the areas and magnification are the same.','1 mark: names the lower surface as the one with more stomata per unit area in this leaf. 1 mark: gives the evidence, the stomata counted in equal areas at the same magnification, with the larger count on the lower impression, so the counts can be compared directly.',True)
e('3.7-13','Using a potometer',[
'A potometer estimates water uptake by measuring movement of a marker in a capillary connected to a leafy shoot in an airtight water-filled apparatus.',
'Water uptake is an indirect estimate of transpiration because some absorbed water is retained or used in the plant.'
],'''Fill the apparatus with water and attach a freshly cut shoot under water. Ensure joints are sealed and the reservoir can reset a small marker bubble. Dry the outside of the leaves. Let the shoot settle under each condition before starting a fixed timing period. Measure bubble displacement along the same capillary; a larger distance per minute means greater uptake if bore area is constant. For a displacement of 18 mm in 3 min, rate = 18 ÷ 3 = 6 mm/min. To calculate volume per time, multiply distance travelled per time by the known capillary cross-sectional area. For a capillary of radius 0.5 mm the area is 3.14 × 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.79 mm², so 6 mm/min corresponds to about 4.7 mm³/min.

Investigate temperature and humidity in separate series: vary one while holding light, wind and the other factor steady. Repeat each setting, reset the bubble and calculate means. Do not count movement caused by leaks as plant uptake. Never allow an air bubble to enter the stem, because it blocks the xylem and stops water uptake.''',src=None)
c('3.7-13','SYNTH','Describe a simple potometer investigation of temperature and humidity effects on water uptake.','Measure marker displacement per minute in a sealed water-filled potometer with a shoot cut under water. Change temperature across a series while holding humidity, light and wind the same; then test humidity while holding temperature and the other conditions constant. Allow adjustment at each setting. Repeat readings, reset the marker and compare means.','1 mark: the independent variables are changed one at a time: temperature in one series and humidity in another. 1 mark: the dependent variable is measured: the distance moved by the marker in a fixed time. 1 mark: the other conditions, such as light and air movement, are kept the same within each series. 1 mark: the shoot is cut and fitted under water, so no air enters the xylem. 1 mark: readings are repeated at each setting and a mean calculated, so the result is reliable.')
c('3.7-13','LIM','Suggest why a leak at a potometer joint makes uptake readings unreliable, and how to improve the apparatus.','Marker movement may include water lost or air entering at the joint rather than uptake by the shoot. Reseal the joint and check that the water-filled apparatus is airtight before repeating.','1 mark: at a leak, water can escape or air can enter, so the marker moves without the shoot taking up that water; this error makes the readings too high or erratic. 1 mark: an improvement is to reseal the joint and check that the apparatus is airtight before repeating the readings.')
c('3.7-13','APP','Describe the uptake pattern from a potometer at fixed temperature: at 35%, 55% and 75% relative humidity the bubble moves 15 mm, 10 mm and 5 mm in 5 min respectively.','Uptake decreases as humidity increases. Bubble speeds are 3, 2 and 1 mm/min respectively.','1 mark: uptake decreases as humidity increases. 1 mark: supports this with rates from the readings, since equal times were used: 3, 2 and 1 mm/min at 35%, 55% and 75% relative humidity.')
e('3.7-14','Wilting',[
'Wilting occurs when cells lose enough water that reduced turgor no longer supports soft plant tissues effectively.',
'Water loss can exceed uptake because of dry soil, rapid transpiration or impaired root and xylem function.'
],'''A flaccid cell presses less firmly against its wall than a turgid one. Many such cells together make a leaf or soft stem droop. Wilting can reduce the leaf area directly exposed to sun and air, but it is a result of water imbalance and not a deliberate muscular movement. Early wilting may be reversed if water supply recovers before permanent damage.

Check both sides of the balance: adding water will help dry soil but will not cure disease-damaged roots or blocked xylem. A plant in waterlogged soil may have little oxygen for root respiration and so take up water poorly despite the abundant water around it. A wilted plant therefore shows that too little water is reaching its cells, which can happen in wet soil as well as dry soil.''')
c('3.7-14','PROC','Describe how a leafy plant wilts when it loses water faster than its roots replace it.','Leaf cells first lose water and turgor. The resulting loss of support makes the leaves droop.','1 mark: leaf cells lose water and become flaccid, losing turgor. 1 mark: then the cells no longer support the leaf, so it droops and the plant wilts.')
c('3.7-14','APP','Suggest a likely explanation for wilting in two plants: one recovers after its dry soil is watered; the other remains wilted in wet soil and has damaged roots.','The first likely lacked soil water and regained turgor after uptake resumed. The second likely cannot take up enough water through its damaged roots despite wet soil.','1 mark: the first plant recovered when watered, so its wilting was caused by lack of water in the dry soil. 1 mark: this second plant has wet soil but damaged roots, so it cannot take up enough water and stays wilted.',demand=inference('Different recovery after watering; root damage established in wet-soil plant.','Distinguish water availability from uptake capacity.'))
e('3.7-15','Xerophytes and local examples',[
'Xerophytes possess features that reduce water loss or help retain a water reserve in dry habitats.',
'Many local aloes have thick waxy leaf surfaces and fleshy leaves; the quiver tree stores water in its stem and branches.',
'Sunken stomata and a small exposed surface area can reduce water loss by keeping humid air near the pores and reducing the evaporating surface.'
],'''A thick waxy cuticle reduces evaporation through the outer surface, and a small exposed surface area reduces the area from which water is lost. Sunken or sheltered stomata keep humid air near the pores, reducing the vapour gradient. Different xerophytes use different combinations of these features.

In a local aloe, fleshy leaves hold a water reserve and a waxy surface helps conserve it. The quiver tree, a southern African succulent, stores water in its stem and branches. Storage helps the plant survive a period when uptake is restricted; it does not itself reduce transpiration.

To compare two plants, link each feature to its effect: for example, a waxy cuticle reduces evaporation from the surface, and sunken stomata trap humid air so the diffusion gradient is smaller. A spiny plant may have a small leaf area, but its stomatal depth can only be judged from a section of the leaf.''',src=None)
c('3.7-15','CHAIN','Discuss three features that help xerophytes such as aloes and the quiver tree conserve water in dry conditions, linking each feature to its effect.','A thick waxy cuticle, as on aloe leaves, reduces evaporation from the surface. Fleshy aloe leaves and the quiver tree’s stem and branches store water, giving a reserve for dry periods. Sunken stomata trap humid air, so the vapour gradient is smaller and less water vapour diffuses out.','1 mark for each of any three features linked to its effect, for example: a thick waxy cuticle reduces evaporation from the surface; fleshy leaves, or a stem and branches, store water so the plant has a reserve when uptake is limited; sunken stomata trap humid air, so the vapour gradient is smaller and less water diffuses out; a small leaf surface area means less area from which water is lost. A feature named without its effect scores nothing.')
c('3.7-15','APP','Suggest which of two equal-area leaves would lose water more slowly under identical dry-air conditions, and why: leaf A has exposed stomata; leaf B has stomata in sheltered pits that retain humid air. Assume equal stomatal opening.','Leaf B. The humid air retained in its pits reduces the vapour gradient, slowing outward diffusion.','1 mark: leaf B loses water more slowly. 1 mark: because in this leaf the humid air held in the pits makes the vapour concentration gradient smaller, so less water vapour diffuses out.',demand=inference('Equal area and opening; different stomatal exposure with humid pits specified.','Infer water-loss difference from boundary conditions rather than plant name.'))
e('3.7-16','Translocation',[
'Translocation is the movement of sucrose and amino acids from the region of production or of storage to a region of utilisation or demand in respiration or growth.',
'Phloem transports these substances from sources to sinks, which can lie above or below the source.'
],'''A mature photosynthesising leaf can supply sucrose to growing roots, buds, flowers or fruits. A stored reserve can later supply new shoots. Source and sink describe current roles, not permanent labels for an organ: a developing storage organ may receive materials, while the same organ later releases stored products. Separate phloem pathways can therefore carry materials in different directions at the same time. Xylem supplies water and mineral ions; it is not the principal route for distributing sucrose.''',src=('SRC-SYL-6116','3.7 Transport in plants: definition of translocation','direct'))
c('3.7-16','DEF','Define translocation.','Movement of sucrose and amino acids from the region of production or of storage to a region of utilisation or demand in respiration or growth.','1 mark: names what is moved, sucrose and amino acids. 1 mark: states that they move from a region of production or storage to a region of use in respiration or growth. The definition must include both points.')
e('3.7-17','Systemic pesticide movement',[
'An absorbed systemic pesticide can be transported internally to tissues beyond the place where it was applied.',
'For a pesticide transported in phloem, translocation distributes it with phloem contents towards receiving tissues.'
],'''A systemic pesticide is absorbed into the plant and then carried through it, so tissues far from where it was applied are also protected. A pesticide carried in the phloem travels with the sucrose and amino acids from the leaves towards growing shoots, roots and other sinks. Insects such as aphids, which feed on phloem sap, then take it in. A contact pesticide acts only where it touches the pest and is not carried through the plant.''',src=None)
c('3.7-17','PROC','Describe how an absorbed systemic pesticide that travels in phloem can reach a growing shoot away from the application site.','The absorbed pesticide enters the phloem. Translocation then carries it with the sucrose and amino acids to the growing shoot, which is a sink.','1 mark: the absorbed pesticide enters the phloem. 1 mark: then it is carried by translocation, with the sucrose and amino acids, to the growing shoot, so it reaches tissue far from where it was applied.')

# CIRCULATORY SYSTEM

e('3.8.1-01','Heart chambers and valves',[
'The mammalian heart has right and left atria receiving blood and right and left ventricles pumping blood out.',
'Valves keep blood flowing forwards, while the septum separates the right and left sides and the thicker left ventricle supplies the body circulation.'
],'''Follow the route rather than memorising a list of names. Blood from the body enters the right atrium through the vena cava, passes to the right ventricle and leaves through the pulmonary artery. Blood from the lungs enters the left atrium through the pulmonary veins, passes to the left ventricle and leaves through the aorta. Atrioventricular valves lie between atria and ventricles; valves at the arterial exits prevent blood returning to the ventricles. Valves open and close according to blood-pressure differences.

The left ventricular wall is thicker because it must generate the force to send blood round the whole body, while the right ventricle only sends blood to the nearby lungs. Both sides pump the same volume over time in a healthy closed circulation. The septum keeps oxygen-rich blood on the left separate from oxygen-poor blood on the right.''',src=H)
c('3.8.1-01','FEATURE','Describe one function of an atrium and one function of a ventricle in the mammalian heart.','An atrium receives returning blood. A ventricle pumps blood into an artery.','1 mark: states one function of an atrium, that it receives blood returning to the heart. 1 mark: states one function of a ventricle, that it pumps blood out into an artery. Each point must be linked to the correct chamber.')
e('3.8.1-02','The external heart',[
'An external mammalian heart view can show the major vessels, the ventricular outline and coronary vessels running across the surface.',
'The anatomical right of a heart faces the viewer’s left in a standard front view, so orientation must be checked before labelling.'
],'''Locate the pointed lower region and the large vessels near the upper region. Distinguish the muscular wall from the coronary vessels on its surface. A drawing of the external surface should show what can be seen externally; do not add internal valve flaps as if the heart were transparent. Draw a clear outline at useful size, keep the major vessel positions accurate and rule label lines to the correct structures. Use the orientation of the specimen you are looking at rather than reversing labels from memory.''',src=H)
c('3.8.1-02','FEATURE','Fig. VIS-6116-3.8.1-02 is a photograph of the external surface of a mammalian heart. Draw a large labelled diagram of the heart as seen in the photograph. Label the aorta, pulmonary artery, vena cava and a coronary vessel.','A large unshaded outline of the heart with the aorta, pulmonary artery and vena cava in their correct positions, and a coronary vessel shown on the surface of the muscular wall, all labelled in the orientation of the photograph.','1 mark: a large, clear, unshaded outline of the heart as it appears in the photograph. 1 mark: the vessels are drawn in the correct positions for that view. 1 mark: any two of the four named structures are labelled correctly, with label lines touching them. 1 mark: all four structures are labelled correctly.',True)
e('3.8.1-03','Arteries',[
'Arteries carry blood away from the heart and have thick muscular and elastic walls suited to relatively high, pulsatile pressure.',
'Elastic recoil helps maintain arterial blood movement between heartbeats; a relatively narrow lumen accompanies the strong wall.'
],'''The defining feature of an artery is the direction of flow, away from the heart, and not the oxygen content of its blood. The pulmonary artery carries blood with relatively little oxygen to the lungs. Elastic tissue stretches as blood is forced out and recoils afterwards, which keeps blood moving between beats. A thick wall withstands the pressure; smooth muscle can alter the vessel diameter.

Link each feature to a job: thick wall, so it does not burst under pressure; elastic tissue, so the pressure pulse is smoothed; smooth muscle, so the diameter and blood flow to an organ can be adjusted. The wall and the tissues in it count as separate features only when each is linked to a different job.''',src=H)
c('3.8.1-03','FEATURE','Outline three structural adaptations of an artery for carrying blood away from the heart under pressure.','A thick wall withstands high pressure without bursting. Elastic tissue stretches and recoils, smoothing the pressure pulse. Smooth muscle in the wall contracts or relaxes to change the lumen diameter and regulate flow.','1 mark each for any three different features, each linked to its function: a thick wall withstands high pressure without bursting; elastic tissue stretches and recoils, smoothing the pulse and keeping blood moving; smooth muscle contracts or relaxes to change the lumen diameter and regulate flow. The wall and the tissues in it count separately only when each is linked to a different function.')
e('3.8.1-04','Veins',[
'Veins carry blood towards the heart at lower pressure than comparable arteries and usually have thinner walls and wider lumens.',
'Valves in many veins help prevent backflow, while contraction of nearby skeletal muscles assists venous return.'
],'''A wide lumen offers a low-resistance route for returning blood. Thick high-pressure walls are less necessary, so the muscular and elastic layers are thinner than in an artery of similar size. Valves are particularly useful in limbs, where blood may return against gravity. Veins are not defined as vessels carrying deoxygenated blood: the pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs.

When leg muscles contract, they squeeze the veins and push blood towards the heart; the valves stop it from flowing back down again. This is why standing still for a long time can make blood collect in the legs, while walking helps blood return.''',src=H)
c('3.8.1-04','FEATURE','Outline three structural features helping a vein return blood at low pressure.','Its wide lumen offers low resistance. Thin walls suit the lower pressure. Valves prevent backflow.','1 mark each for three features linked to function: a wide lumen gives low resistance to flow; thin walls are enough because the pressure is low; valves prevent backflow. Squeezing by nearby muscles is not a feature of the vein itself, so it earns no extra mark.')
e('3.8.1-05','Capillaries',[
'Capillaries form extensive networks connecting small arteries to small veins near body cells.',
'A capillary wall is one cell thick and its narrow lumen brings blood close to the exchange surface.'
],'''Many branching capillaries provide a large total exchange area. Short distances between blood and cells favour diffusion, and the thin wall shortens that path further. Oxygen and dissolved nutrients can leave the blood for tissue fluid, while carbon dioxide and other products move towards the blood. A capillary is an exchange vessel, not a tiny artery with a thick muscular coat.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','40.4 Blood Flow and Blood Pressure: capillary exchange','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.1-05','FEATURE','Outline three adaptations of capillaries for exchanging substances with tissues.','A one-cell-thick wall gives a short diffusion distance. Extensive branching gives a large total exchange area. A narrow lumen keeps blood close to the wall.','1 mark each for three different points linking a structure to its function: a wall one cell thick gives a short diffusion distance; extensive branching gives a large surface area for exchange; a narrow lumen keeps blood close to the wall. Each point must say what the feature does.')
e('3.8.1-06','Double circulation',[
'In double circulation, blood passes through the heart twice during a complete journey through lungs and body.',
'The pulmonary circuit runs from heart to lungs and back; the systemic circuit runs from heart to body tissues and back.'
],'''The right ventricle supplies the pulmonary circuit and the left supplies the systemic circuit. At the lungs the blood gains oxygen and loses carbon dioxide; in most tissues the reverse net exchange occurs. Separation allows the body to receive oxygen-rich blood at a suitable pressure while the lung circuit operates at a lower pressure, which protects the delicate capillaries in the lungs. The circuits are joined in series, so the whole output of one side becomes the input of the other.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','40.1 Overview of the Circulatory System: double circulation','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.1-06','FEATURE','Describe the two circuits in mammalian double circulation.','Pulmonary: heart → lungs → heart. Systemic: heart → body tissues → heart.','1 mark: states the pulmonary circuit, from the heart to the lungs and back to the heart. 1 mark: states the systemic circuit, from the heart to the body tissues and back to the heart. Each point must name the circuit and both ends of its route.')
e('3.8.1-07','Pulse and activity',[
'A pulse is an arterial pressure wave associated with ventricular ejection and can estimate heart rate by counting beats over time.',
'Moderate exercise commonly increases pulse rate, helping deliver more oxygen and nutrients and remove respiratory products from active tissues.'
],'''In a supervised voluntary investigation, let a healthy participant rest before counting the wrist pulse for a full minute. Record beats/min. Use a defined comfortable activity such as stepping at a set pace for a short time; measure promptly afterwards and at fixed recovery times. Keep activity duration, timing and posture consistent. Repeat after full recovery rather than comparing a fresh first trial with a fatigued second trial. Participation must be optional, within school safety arrangements, and stopped if discomfort occurs.

A resting count of 72 beats/min and an immediate post-activity count of 108 beats/min show a rise of 36 beats/min. A reading taken two minutes later measures recovery, not the immediate response. Measurements from one student cannot give a population norm or diagnose disease.''',src=None)
c('3.8.1-07','SYNTH','Describe a safe investigation of the effect of moderate activity on pulse rate.','Change rest versus a defined comfortable activity. Measure wrist pulse for a fixed interval and express beats/min. Obtain a rested baseline, perform the same brief activity, then count promptly and at fixed recovery times. Keep participant, activity duration and timing the same. Repeat after full recovery; participation is voluntary and stops with discomfort.','1 mark: the independent variable is changed: rest compared with a defined comfortable activity. 1 mark: the dependent variable is measured: pulse counted for a fixed time and given as beats/min. 1 mark: at least two variables are kept the same, such as the participant, the activity, its duration and the timing of the count. 1 mark: a rested baseline count is taken before the activity and further counts at fixed times after it. 1 mark: the investigation is repeated after full recovery and a mean calculated, so the result is reliable. Safe practice (voluntary participation, stopping on discomfort) is expected but not separately credited.')
c('3.8.1-07','LIM','Suggest the error in counting a pulse for only 5 s and multiplying the count, and an improvement.','A one-beat counting error becomes large after scaling to a minute. Count for a longer standard interval, preferably a full minute when feasible.','1 mark: a miscount of one beat in 5 s becomes an error of 12 beats/min when the count is multiplied by 12. 1 mark: an improvement is to count for a longer time, such as a full minute.')
c('3.8.1-07','APP','State two conclusions from these wrist-pulse readings for one student: rested 70 beats/min; immediately after stepping 112 beats/min; after 4 min recovery 74 beats/min.','Pulse increased after activity. During recovery it returned towards, but had not exactly reached, the resting rate.','1 mark: the pulse rate rose after activity, from 70 to 112 beats/min. 1 mark: after 4 min it had fallen to 74 beats/min, close to but still above this student’s resting rate of 70, so recovery was not quite complete.')
# Risk factors: each is probabilistic; separate causes from associations.
risk=[
('08','Diet and coronary disease',['A diet high in saturated fat can increase the risk of fatty deposits narrowing coronary arteries.','A coronary artery blockage deprives part of the heart muscle of oxygen and may cause a heart attack.'],'''Narrowing limits blood supply; a clot forming at a diseased area can reduce it sharply. The problem lies in the coronary vessels that supply the heart muscle, not in a blocked heart chamber. Diet affects risk over a long time, so one meal does not cause an attack. Risk also depends on other factors. A balanced diet can reduce risk, though it cannot guarantee prevention.''','Describe how a diet high in saturated fat can contribute to a heart attack.','It can favour fatty deposits that narrow coronary arteries. Reduced coronary blood flow can deprive heart muscle of oxygen.','1 mark: fatty deposits build up in the coronary arteries and narrow them. 1 mark: less blood, and so less oxygen, reaches the heart muscle, which can cause a heart attack. Each mark is for a separate point.','Explain why narrowing a coronary artery can damage the heart although blood still fills its chambers.','Coronary arteries supply the muscle itself. Blood in chambers cannot replace an inadequate supply through those vessels.','1 mark: gives the reason that the coronary arteries are the vessels that supply the heart muscle itself. 1 mark: blood inside the chambers does not supply the muscle wall, so narrowed coronary arteries still starve it of oxygen.'),
('09','Smoking and coronary disease',['Smoking increases coronary heart disease risk through effects including blood-vessel damage and a greater tendency for clots to form.','Carbon monoxide in smoke reduces oxygen transport, while nicotine increases cardiovascular workload.'],'''Smoking raises the chance of coronary disease but does not make it certain for any one person. Less oxygen carried in the blood and a reduced coronary supply act together. Nicotine can raise heart rate and cause vessels to constrict. These effects on the heart and blood vessels are different from tar damage in the airways, which is taught in 3.9.''','Describe two ways smoking can increase strain on the heart or impair its oxygen supply.','Nicotine can increase heart rate and narrow vessels. Carbon monoxide reduces the oxygen the blood can carry.','1 mark each for any two separate points: nicotine increases heart rate; nicotine narrows blood vessels; carbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen; smoke damages artery linings, making clots more likely.','Explain why carbon monoxide can worsen the effects of narrowed coronary arteries.','The narrowed artery already restricts blood supply. Carbon monoxide lowers the oxygen carried by the blood that still reaches the muscle.','1 mark: gives the reason that the narrowed artery already limits the blood supply to the heart muscle. 1 mark: because carbon monoxide reduces the oxygen carried by each volume of blood, the muscle receives even less oxygen.'),
('10','Stress and coronary risk',['Persistent stress can contribute to coronary risk through sustained cardiovascular strain and associated behaviours.','Stress responses can raise heart rate and blood pressure, increasing the heart’s workload.'],'''Stress is one influence among several. A short-lived stress response is normal and healthy; the risk comes from stress that is persistent, because the raised heart rate and blood pressure are then sustained. Persistent stress can also lead to behaviours that raise risk, such as smoking or eating a poor diet. Stress alone does not explain a heart attack.''','Describe how persistent stress may increase coronary disease risk.','Repeated stress responses can raise blood pressure. The resulting sustained workload strains the cardiovascular system.','1 mark: states that repeated stress responses raise heart rate and blood pressure. 1 mark: states that the sustained extra workload strains the heart and arteries, which increases the risk of coronary disease. Each mark is for a separate point.','Explain why persistent stress may place extra demands on heart muscle.','Stress responses can raise heart rate and blood pressure. The heart therefore works harder and needs more oxygen.','1 mark: gives the reason that stress responses raise heart rate and blood pressure. 1 mark: so the heart works harder and needs more oxygen, which means greater demand on the coronary blood supply.'),
('11','Age and coronary risk',['Coronary heart disease becomes more common with increasing age, although it can occur in younger people.','Older people have generally had longer exposure to factors that damage arteries or favour fatty deposits.'],'''Age is linked to risk but is not itself the blockage. Arterial changes accumulate over many years, so older people have on average had longer exposure to damaging factors. The extent of change differs greatly between people. Younger people can also develop coronary disease, particularly with other risk factors such as smoking or a diet high in saturated fat.

A population trend describes how risk changes for groups of people; it does not predict that every older person has narrowed coronary arteries.''','Describe the relationship between increasing age and heart-attack risk.','Risk tends to increase with age. Longer exposure to arterial damage and deposit-forming factors can contribute.','1 mark: states that the risk of a heart attack increases with age. 1 mark: states a contributing point, that longer exposure to artery-damaging factors such as fatty deposits adds up over the years.','Explain why age-related coronary risk does not mean every older person will have a heart attack.','Age is a risk association, not a certainty. Individuals differ in arterial health and other risk factors.','1 mark: gives the reason that age is a risk factor and not a certainty. 1 mark: individuals differ in arterial health and in other risk factors, so many older people never have a heart attack.'),
('12','Sex-related coronary risk',['Coronary disease risk differs between male and female populations, with males tending to develop it earlier on average.','Population differences in risk do not establish whether a particular individual will develop coronary disease.'],'''This risk factor is often called gender in textbooks and examinations. The biological comparison is between male and female populations: on average, males tend to develop coronary disease earlier in life. Females can also develop coronary disease, and the difference becomes smaller with age.''','Describe the usual population relationship between sex and early coronary disease risk, including a qualification.','Males tend to develop coronary disease earlier on average. Females can also be affected, and individual risk depends on other factors.','1 mark: states that males tend to develop coronary disease earlier than females, on average. 1 mark: gives a qualification, such as that females can also be affected or that individual risk depends on other factors. Each mark is for a separate point.','Explain why sex alone cannot establish whether an individual will have a heart attack.','The sex-related difference describes population risk. Other factors and individual arterial health vary within each group.','1 mark: gives the reason that the difference describes populations, not individuals. 1 mark: other factors and arterial health vary within each group, so sex alone cannot predict one person’s outcome.')]
for n,h,claims,x,p,a,g1,p2,a2,g2 in risk:
 o='3.8.1-'+n;e(o,h,claims,x,src=None)
 c(o,'FEATURE',p,a,g1)
 c(o,'WHY',p2,a2,g2)
for n,h,claims,x,p,answer,g in [
('13','Aspirin',['Aspirin can reduce platelet aggregation and hence reduce clot formation in coronary disease treatment.','Treatment choice depends on clinical assessment because aspirin also increases bleeding risk.'],'Aspirin is prescribed by doctors after an assessment, because it also increases the risk of bleeding. It makes platelets less likely to clump together, so clots are less likely to block a coronary artery. It does not remove fatty deposits that are already present and does not repair heart muscle that has been starved of oxygen.','State how aspirin can help in treating coronary heart disease.','It reduces platelet aggregation, lowering the chance of a coronary clot.','1 mark: one correct fact, that aspirin reduces the clumping of platelets so a clot is less likely to form in the coronary arteries.'),
('14','Stents',['A coronary stent is a small mesh tube used to support a narrowed artery in an open position.','Keeping the artery open can improve blood delivery to the heart muscle.'],'A stent supports the artery wall from the inside. It keeps the narrowed vessel open so that blood can flow through it normally; it is not an alternative route around the narrowing. A stent treats the narrowed region but does not remove the causes of coronary disease, so diet, smoking and exercise still matter.','State the role of a stent in treating coronary heart disease.','It holds the narrowed coronary artery open to improve blood flow.','1 mark: one correct point, that the stent holds the narrowed coronary artery open so blood flow to the heart muscle improves.'),
('15','Angioplasty',['Coronary angioplasty uses an inflated balloon to widen a narrowed region of a coronary artery.','A stent may be placed during the procedure to help keep the artery open.'],'In angioplasty a thin tube with a small balloon is guided to the narrowed place. Inflating the balloon widens the existing route. A stent can be left in place afterwards to keep the vessel open. Angioplasty is different from by-pass surgery, which provides a new route.','State what angioplasty does to a narrowed coronary artery.','A balloon is inflated inside it to widen the narrowed region.','1 mark: one correct fact, that a balloon is inflated inside the narrowed coronary artery to widen it.'),
('16','By-pass surgery',['Coronary by-pass surgery creates an alternative blood route around a narrowed or blocked coronary region.','The alternative route improves delivery to the heart muscle beyond the obstruction.'],'In by-pass surgery a surgeon joins a piece of vessel, often taken from the leg or chest, to the coronary artery before and after the blockage. Blood then flows around the obstruction to reach the heart muscle. The operation does not by-pass the heart itself. Changes to diet, smoking and exercise are still needed afterwards to slow further narrowing.','State how coronary by-pass surgery improves blood supply.','A grafted vessel carries blood around a narrowed coronary region.','1 mark: one correct point, that a grafted vessel carries blood around the narrowed or blocked part of the coronary artery.')]:
 o='3.8.1-'+n;e(o,h,claims,x,src=None);c(o,'FEATURE',p,answer,g)
e('3.8.1-17','Lymphatic vessels and nodes',[
'The lymphatic system includes lymphatic vessels that carry lymph and lymph nodes along their routes.',
'Lymph is fluid within lymphatic vessels; it originates as tissue fluid around cells.'
],'''Lymph nodes are small structures found at intervals along lymphatic vessels. They contain white blood cells and are not the same as endocrine glands, which secrete hormones. Lymphatic vessels eventually return collected fluid to the blood. The system has two roles, fluid circulation and defence, which the next two sections develop.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','40.4 Blood Flow and Blood Pressure: lymphatic system','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.1-17','FEATURE','State the name of the small structures found at intervals along lymphatic vessels that filter lymph.','Lymph nodes.','1 mark: names the structures as lymph nodes. No mark for naming the vessels or the fluid.')
e('3.8.1-18','Lymph nodes and defence',[
'Lymph nodes filter lymph and bring carried foreign material into contact with immune cells.',
'Lymphocytes and phagocytic cells in lymphatic tissues contribute to defence against infection.'
],'''Fluid draining from tissues can carry pathogens or their components. As it passes through nodes, cells can recognise foreign material and start the appropriate response. Lymphocytes take part in specific antibody responses; phagocytes engulf material. Nodes are therefore active defence sites and not simply strainers that catch every pathogen.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','42.1 Innate Immune Response: lymphatic system','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.1-18','FEATURE','Describe two ways lymph nodes help defend against infection.','They filter material carried in lymph. They contain immune cells that recognise or destroy pathogens.','1 mark: states that the nodes filter the lymph, trapping foreign material or pathogens carried in it. 1 mark: states that the nodes contain white blood cells, lymphocytes or phagocytes, that recognise or destroy pathogens. Each mark is for a separate point.')
c('3.8.1-18','WHY','Explain why lymph from an infected tissue passes through lymph nodes before returning to the blood.','It carries material from the infected area. In nodes, that material encounters immune cells that can respond to it.','1 mark: gives the reason that lymph from the infected tissue carries pathogens or foreign material. 1 mark: states the purpose, that in the nodes this material meets white blood cells that can respond to it before the lymph rejoins the blood.')
e('3.8.1-19','Returning tissue fluid',[
'Some fluid surrounding body cells enters lymphatic vessels and becomes lymph.',
'The lymphatic system returns this fluid to the bloodstream, helping prevent its accumulation in tissues.'
],'''Tissue fluid bathes cells and allows exchange between them and capillary blood. Much returns directly to capillaries; the remaining fluid can drain into lymph vessels. Valves and body movement assist lymph flow towards its return to the blood. If drainage is impaired, fluid can build up and cause swelling. The circulation of body fluid is therefore connected with, but different from, immune recognition in nodes.

For example, a person who sits for many hours on a long flight may find their ankles swell, because without muscle movement tissue fluid is not drained and returned efficiently.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','40.4 Blood Flow and Blood Pressure: lymphatic system','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.1-19','FEATURE','Describe the lymphatic system’s role in circulating body fluid.','It collects excess tissue fluid as lymph. It returns that fluid to the blood.','1 mark: states that it collects excess tissue fluid from around the cells as lymph. 1 mark: states that it returns this fluid to the blood. Each mark is for a separate point.')
c('3.8.1-19','WHY','Explain why poor lymph drainage can cause tissue swelling.','Excess tissue fluid is not returned efficiently to the blood. Fluid accumulates around the cells.','1 mark: gives the reason that excess tissue fluid is not returned efficiently to the blood. 1 mark: so the fluid builds up around the cells, which is seen as swelling.')

# BLOOD

e('3.8.2-01','Recognising blood cells',[
'Mature human red blood cells are biconcave discs without nuclei, while white blood cells have nuclei and are generally larger.',
'A lymphocyte typically has a large rounded nucleus occupying much of the cell; a phagocyte can have a lobed nucleus.'
],'''In a stained blood film, many small pale-centred discs are red cells. Look for a stained nucleus inside a larger cell before calling it a white cell. A pale central depression in a red cell is not a nucleus. Stain intensity and colour alone are unreliable identifiers. Use size, shape and nuclear appearance together.

The two main groups of white cell look different. A lymphocyte has a large rounded nucleus filling most of the cell, with little cytoplasm. A phagocyte has a lobed nucleus and more cytoplasm. A blood film from a healthy person contains far more red cells than white cells, so a field with no white cell is normal and does not mean the cells are absent from the body.

To identify a cell, ask in order: does it have a nucleus? If not, it is a red cell. If it does, is the nucleus rounded and large (lymphocyte) or lobed (phagocyte)?''',src=B)
c('3.8.2-01','FEATURE','Fig. VIS-6116-3.8.2-01 is a photomicrograph of a stained human blood film. Identify one red blood cell and one white blood cell, using the labels on the figure.','The red blood cell is a small biconcave disc with no nucleus. The white blood cell is a larger cell with a stained nucleus.','1 mark: names a labelled cell without a nucleus, a small biconcave disc, as the red blood cell. 1 mark: names a labelled larger cell with a stained nucleus as the white blood cell. Each point is for one correctly identified cell, judged by its nuclear evidence.',True)
c('3.8.2-01','MISCON','Fig. VIS-6116-3.8.2-01 is a photomicrograph of a stained human blood film. A learner says that the cell with the large dark nucleus is a red blood cell. Explain the error and name the correct group of cells.','Mature human red blood cells have no nucleus. A cell with a nucleus belongs to the white blood cell group.','1 mark: explains the error, that mature human red blood cells have no nucleus, so a cell with a large nucleus cannot be one. 1 mark: names the correct group, white blood cells, instead.',True)
e('3.8.2-02','Red blood cell function',[
'Red blood cells transport oxygen using haemoglobin.',
'The biconcave form provides a large surface area relative to volume and a short path for oxygen exchange.'
],'''Haemoglobin combines reversibly with oxygen, loading where oxygen is abundant and releasing it in tissues with lower oxygen concentration. The lack of a nucleus leaves more space for haemoglobin in mature human red cells. Oxygen transport is different from the release of energy in cellular respiration, which happens inside all living cells.''',src=B)
c('3.8.2-02','FEATURE','State the main function of red blood cells.','They transport oxygen using haemoglobin.','1 mark: states one correct function, the transport of oxygen (by haemoglobin).')
e('3.8.2-03','White blood cell function',[
'White blood cells defend the body against pathogens.',
'Different white cells contribute through mechanisms including phagocytosis and antibody production.'
],'''White blood cells form several groups, so it is too broad to say that every white cell produces antibodies. Lymphocytes and phagocytes show two different defence functions: lymphocytes produce antibodies, while phagocytes engulf and digest pathogens. The oxygen-carrying job belongs to red cells and is not a function of white cells.''',src=B)
c('3.8.2-03','FEATURE','State the general function of white blood cells.','Defence against pathogens.','1 mark: states one correct function, defence of the body against pathogens (disease-causing organisms).')
e('3.8.2-04','Platelet function',[
'Platelets are cell fragments involved in starting blood-clot formation at a damaged vessel.',
'The resulting clot helps seal the damaged region and reduce blood loss.'
],'''Platelets are fragments of larger cells and are much smaller than red or white blood cells. They are not a third kind of white blood cell. At a damaged vessel they gather and help start clotting, which leads to the formation of a mesh of fibrin described later in this unit.''',src=B)
c('3.8.2-04','FEATURE','State the function of platelets.','They help initiate blood clotting at a damaged vessel.','1 mark: states one correct function, that platelets help to start blood clotting at a damaged vessel.')
e('3.8.2-05','Plasma transport',[
'Plasma is the liquid component carrying blood cells and dissolved substances.',
'Plasma transports substances including glucose, amino acids, carbon dioxide, urea and hormones, and distributes heat.'
],'''When asked for an example of what plasma carries, choose a named cargo. Oxygen is carried mainly by haemoglobin inside red cells, whereas dissolved nutrients such as glucose and amino acids travel in the plasma, as do urea from the liver to the kidneys and hormones from glands to target organs. Plasma also spreads heat from active organs round the body. Plasma is not the same as tissue fluid, though the two fluids exchange materials at capillaries.''',src=B)
c('3.8.2-05','FEATURE','State one transport function of blood plasma other than moving blood cells.','It carries dissolved nutrients such as glucose.','1 mark: one correct fact, that plasma carries a named substance such as glucose, amino acids, carbon dioxide, urea or hormones, or that it distributes heat.')
e('3.8.2-06','Lymphocytes',[
'Lymphocytes participate in specific immune responses, including antibody production.',
'A typical lymphocyte image shows a large rounded nucleus with a relatively small surrounding amount of cytoplasm.'
],'''Antibodies recognise particular antigens; this is different from engulfing a whole pathogen. Some lymphocytes form memory cells after active responses. In a stained blood film, a lymphocyte is identified by its large rounded nucleus and thin rim of cytoplasm. It can be told apart from a phagocyte, which has a lobed nucleus and more cytoplasm.''',src=I)
c('3.8.2-06','FEATURE','Fig. VIS-6116-3.8.2-06 is a labelled diagram of blood cells. Cell L has a large rounded nucleus that fills most of the cell, with very little cytoplasm. State the defence function of cell L.','Cell L is a lymphocyte. It produces antibodies as part of a specific immune response.','1 mark: states the function of a lymphocyte, the production of antibodies against specific antigens. The cell type is not named in the figure, so the function itself must be given; engulfing pathogens is not credited for this cell.',True)
e('3.8.2-07','Phagocytes',[
'Phagocytes engulf pathogens and digest the engulfed material.',
'The cell membrane extends around the pathogen to enclose it before digestion occurs inside the phagocyte.'
],'''A lobed nucleus helps to identify a common phagocyte in a stained blood film. The phagocyte changes shape, extending its membrane around the pathogen until it is enclosed, and then digests it. Engulfing is different from antibody secretion, which is the job of lymphocytes, and red cells have no part in either process.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','42.1 Innate Immune Response: phagocytic cells','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.2-07','FEATURE','Fig. VIS-6116-3.8.2-07 is a labelled diagram of blood cells. Cell P has a lobed nucleus and a large amount of cytoplasm. State the defence function of cell P.','Cell P is a phagocyte. It engulfs and digests pathogens (phagocytosis).','1 mark: states the function of a phagocyte, that it engulfs pathogens and digests them. Engulfing or ingesting alone is accepted; antibody production is not credited for this cell.',True)
e('3.8.2-08','Fibrin and clot formation',[
'During clotting, soluble fibrinogen is converted to insoluble fibrin.',
'Fibrin forms fibres that make a mesh trapping blood cells and helping form a clot.'
],'''At a vessel injury, platelets gather and the clotting process leads to fibrin formation. Fibrinogen is the dissolved starting material, not the final fibre network. The fibres retain cells and platelets at the wound; the resulting plug limits further bleeding. This is different from antibody production in the immune response.''',src=B)
c('3.8.2-08','PROC','Describe the conversion and immediate result involving fibrinogen during blood clotting.','First, soluble fibrinogen becomes insoluble fibrin. Then fibrin fibres form a mesh trapping blood cells.','1 mark: soluble fibrinogen is converted to insoluble fibrin. 1 mark: then the fibrin forms a mesh of fibres that traps blood cells, so a clot forms.')
c('3.8.2-08','MECH','Describe the complete clot-forming sequence from a damaged vessel, including the roles of platelets, fibrinogen and fibrin.','Platelets gather at the vessel damage. Clotting leads to soluble fibrinogen becoming insoluble fibrin. Fibrin fibres form a mesh trapping blood cells. This produces a clot sealing the damaged region.','1 mark: platelets gather at the damaged vessel and start clotting. 1 mark: then soluble fibrinogen is converted to insoluble fibrin. 1 mark: the fibrin forms a mesh of fibres that traps blood cells. 1 mark: so a clot forms that seals the damaged vessel and reduces blood loss.')
e('3.8.2-09','Why blood clots',[
'A clot reduces loss of blood from a damaged vessel.',
'Sealing a wound also reduces entry of pathogens through the damaged surface.'
],'''Clotting is useful at an injury, but a clot forming inside an intact coronary vessel can deprive heart muscle of oxygen. The same plugging action has different consequences at these different sites.''',src=B)
c('3.8.2-09','FEATURE','State one protective role of blood clotting at a cut.','It reduces blood loss by sealing the damaged vessel.','1 mark: one correct point, that clotting reduces blood loss by sealing the damaged vessel, or that it reduces the entry of pathogens.')
e('3.8.2-10','Capillaries and tissue fluid',[
'Tissue fluid surrounds body cells and provides an intermediate exchange medium between blood and cells.',
'Dissolved nutrients move from capillary blood into tissue fluid and then enter cells; metabolic products can move in the opposite direction.'
],'''Plasma supplies dissolved nutrients to the capillary exchange region. Cells take up nutrients from their immediate surroundings, not by direct contact with a circulating red cell. Oxygen also diffuses from blood towards respiring tissues, while carbon dioxide moves back towards the capillaries. Some tissue fluid returns to the blood and some drains through lymph vessels. Tissue fluid contains far fewer large blood components than whole blood, because blood cells and large proteins stay inside the capillaries.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','40.4 Blood Flow and Blood Pressure: capillary exchange','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.2-10','PROC','Describe the route by which dissolved glucose in capillary blood reaches a body cell.','It first passes from capillary blood into tissue fluid. It then crosses into the body cell from that fluid.','1 mark: glucose passes out of the capillary blood into the tissue fluid. 1 mark: then it passes from the tissue fluid into the body cell, so it reaches the cell.')

# BODY DEFENCE

e('3.8.3-01','Pathogens',[
'A pathogen is a disease causing organism.',
'Pathogens can enter a host and disrupt normal function; not every microorganism is pathogenic.'
],'''A pathogen is an organism that causes disease. The disease is the resulting disturbance of normal function, and the pathogen is the cause. A nutrient deficiency can also cause disease but is not a pathogen, because nothing is infecting the body. Many bacteria are harmless or useful, such as those in the gut that help with digestion.

Identify whether a question asks about a disease-causing agent, a symptom or a non-infectious condition before naming a pathogen. Examples of pathogens are the bacterium that causes cholera, the virus that causes influenza and the protozoan that causes malaria.''',src=('SRC-SYL-6116','3.8.3 Body defence: pathogen definition','direct'))
c('3.8.3-01','DEF','Define pathogen.','A disease causing organism.','1 mark: states that a pathogen is an organism. 1 mark: states that it causes disease. The definition must include both points.')
e('3.8.3-02','Active immunity',[
'Active immunity is defence against a pathogen by antibody production in the body.',
'Active responses can produce memory cells that enable a faster response on later exposure to the same antigen.'
],'''Antibodies are proteins that recognise particular antigens, which are identifying molecules on foreign material. Active describes the person's own response; it has nothing to do with exercise or how severe the illness is. Infection or vaccination can stimulate this response. Antibody production takes time during a first encounter; an existing memory response is faster. Immunity can reduce the chance or severity of disease, but protection is not always complete or lifelong.

Compare a first and second infection: after the first exposure, antibodies take several days to reach a useful level, and the person may be ill meanwhile. After a second exposure to the same pathogen, memory cells start antibody production sooner and make more of it, so the person may not become ill at all.''',src=('SRC-SYL-6116','3.8.3 Body defence: active immunity definition','direct'))
c('3.8.3-02','DEF','Define active immunity.','Defence against a pathogen by antibody production in the body.','1 mark: defence against a pathogen. 1 mark: by antibodies made in the person’s own body. The definition must include both points.')
e('3.8.3-03','Mechanical barriers',[
'Intact skin forms a physical barrier that restricts entry of pathogens into the body.',
'Nasal hairs trap some particles before they travel further into the air passages.'
],'''A barrier acts before a pathogen reaches internal tissues. Damage such as a cut interrupts skin protection; clotting and healing help close the entry route. Nasal hairs act by trapping particles, and they do not produce antibodies. The natural mechanical barriers of the body are different from manufactured barriers such as condoms, which reduce the spread of some infections but are not part of the body's defences.

Mechanical barriers stop pathogens physically. Chemical barriers, such as stomach acid, destroy them. When a question asks for a mechanical barrier, name a physical structure such as skin, not an acid or an enzyme.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','42.1 Innate Immune Response: physical barriers','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.3-03','FEATURE','State one natural mechanical barrier that helps defend the body against pathogens.','Intact skin.','1 mark: names one natural mechanical barrier of the body, such as intact skin or nasal hairs.')
c('3.8.3-03','MISCON','A learner is asked for one natural mechanical barrier against pathogens and writes "a condom". Explain the error and give a correct example.','A condom is a manufactured barrier used in contraception, not a natural part of the body’s defences. Intact skin is a natural barrier that stops pathogens entering.','1 mark: explains the error, that a condom is a manufactured barrier and not a natural defence of the body. 1 mark: gives a correct natural example, such as intact skin or nasal hairs, instead.')
e('3.8.3-04','Chemical barriers',[
'Acid in the stomach can destroy many pathogens swallowed with food.',
'Chemical defences can act before a specific antibody response develops.'
],'''Stomach acid provides a hostile chemical environment that destroys many pathogens swallowed with food or drink. It is not an antibody, and it protects only the stomach and the gut beyond it. A barrier lowers the number of living pathogens reaching the tissues but does not remove all risk. Stomach acid destroys pathogens chemically, whereas nasal hairs trap them physically.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','42.1 Innate Immune Response: chemical barriers','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.3-04','FEATURE','State one chemical barrier in the body that can destroy swallowed pathogens.','Stomach acid.','1 mark: names one chemical barrier, the acid in the stomach.')
e('3.8.3-05','Antibody production',[
'Antibodies recognise and bind particular antigens.',
'Binding can neutralise harmful material or help other defence mechanisms remove it.'
],'''Antibodies are specific. An antibody that binds one antigen does not usually fit a different one, so a response to one pathogen generally does not protect against another. Lymphocytes produce antibodies, unlike red cells, which transport oxygen. An antibody is a protein, not a cell, and it cannot engulf bacteria. The immune response involves cooperation between cells and soluble molecules: antibodies can also make pathogens easier for phagocytes to recognise and engulf.''',src=I)
c('3.8.3-05','FEATURE','State how antibody production contributes to defence.','It provides antibodies that bind specific antigens and help neutralise or remove the pathogen.','1 mark: one correct point, that antibodies bind to specific antigens and so help to neutralise or remove the pathogen.')
e('3.8.3-06','Phagocytosis',[
'Phagocytosis involves a white blood cell surrounding and engulfing a pathogen.',
'The engulfed material is enclosed inside the cell and digested.'
],'''The phagocyte changes shape around the pathogen: its membrane flows round it until it is enclosed in a vesicle inside the cell. Digestion follows enclosure. This cellular defence differs from antibodies binding antigens outside cells, though antibodies can help phagocytes recognise targets. Red blood cells have no digestive role.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','42.1 Innate Immune Response: phagocytosis','paraphrase'))
c('3.8.3-06','FEATURE','State how a phagocyte defends the body.','It engulfs and digests pathogens.','1 mark: one complete fact, that a phagocyte engulfs pathogens and digests them.')
e('3.8.3-07','Vaccination and spread',[
'Vaccination exposes the immune system to suitable antigens without requiring the person to undergo the corresponding full disease.',
'An effective vaccine stimulates an active response and memory, so later exposure can be controlled more quickly and transmission may be reduced.'
],'''A vaccine can contain weakened or inactive material, or selected components, depending on its type. It is not the same as an injection of ready-made antibodies. With more people protected, a pathogen meets fewer hosts in which it can multiply and spread. How well a vaccine protects depends on the vaccine, the timing and the proportion of people vaccinated. Some vaccines need later doses to build lasting memory.

A vaccinated person can sometimes still be infected and pass the pathogen on, so health services also monitor cases of disease.

For example, imagine two similar villages. In one, 90 out of 100 people are vaccinated; in the other, 20 out of 100. When one infected person arrives, in the first village most contacts are likely to resist infection and the outbreak is likely to die out, while in the second the pathogen finds many susceptible people and spreads. A community can still contain susceptible people after a successful campaign, which is why high coverage matters.''',src=I)
c('3.8.3-07','PROC','Explain how vaccination can reduce the spread of an infectious disease.','Vaccination first stimulates a specific immune response and memory. Later exposure is controlled more quickly, so fewer successful infections can reduce transmission.','1 mark: vaccination stimulates a specific immune response and the formation of memory cells. 1 mark: then on later exposure the pathogen is destroyed quickly, so fewer people become infected and fewer pass it on.')
c('3.8.3-07','APP','Explain a likely reason why an outbreak stops in a dormitory where most contacts were vaccinated in advance but continues in a comparable dormitory with few vaccinated contacts. Exposure and crowding are similar.','More contacts in the first dormitory have immune memory and resist infection more effectively. Fewer successful infections reduce opportunities for onward transmission.','1 mark: more of the contacts in the first dormitory have memory cells from vaccination, so they resist infection. 1 mark: this means fewer people become infected and pass the pathogen on, so the outbreak ends.',demand=inference('Comparable exposure/crowding but different previous vaccination and onward spread.','Infer a protection/transmission link from the controlled contrast.'))
e('3.8.3-08','Passive immunity',[
'Passive immunity is a short-term defence against a pathogen by antibodies acquired from another individual.',
'Ready-made antibodies can act promptly but do not by themselves establish the recipient’s immune memory.'
],'''The recipient receives an existing defensive product rather than first making it. Examples include maternal antibody transfer and medically supplied antibodies. Passive refers to the source of the antibodies, not to an inactive child or a weak response. How long the protection lasts depends on how long the supplied antibodies survive in the body, usually weeks or months.

Active and passive immunity can be compared by source and duration: in active immunity the person makes antibodies and memory cells, and protection can last for years; in passive immunity the antibodies come from someone else and protection is short.''',src=('SRC-SYL-6116','3.8.3 Body defence: passive immunity definition','direct'))
c('3.8.3-08','DEF','Define passive immunity.','A short-term defence against a pathogen by antibodies acquired from another individual.','1 mark: short-term defence against a pathogen. 1 mark: by antibodies acquired from another individual. The definition must include both points.')
e('3.8.3-09','Establishing active immunity',[
'On first exposure to an appropriate antigen, specific lymphocytes are activated and produce an antibody response.',
'Some responding lymphocytes become memory cells, allowing a faster and stronger response to a later encounter with the same antigen.'
],'''Follow the causal order: an antigen is recognised, matching cells respond, antibodies become available and memory cells remain. Antibodies bind their matching antigen; they do not become memory cells. On a second encounter the remembered antigen triggers rapid production, which can control the pathogen before substantial illness develops. The first response can come from natural infection or vaccination. A person need not experience the full disease to gain active immunity through a vaccine. The first response is slow, while the response of memory cells is much faster.''',src=I)
c('3.8.3-09','PROC','Describe the two lasting outcomes of an active response to a suitable antigen.','The body produces specific antibodies. Some activated lymphocytes then form memory cells for a quicker later response.','1 mark: the body produces specific antibodies against the antigen. 1 mark: then some of the activated lymphocytes become memory cells, so a later response is quicker.')
c('3.8.3-09','MECH','Describe the complete sequence by which exposure to a suitable antigen leads to active immunity and a quicker response on later exposure.','Specific lymphocytes recognise the antigen. They multiply and produce matching antibodies. Some cells remain as memory cells. Later encounter with the same antigen triggers faster antibody production and control.','1 mark: specific lymphocytes recognise the antigen. 1 mark: then they multiply and produce matching antibodies. 1 mark: some of these cells remain as memory cells. 1 mark: so on later exposure antibodies are produced faster and in larger amounts, and the pathogen is controlled quickly.')
e('3.8.3-10','Acquiring passive protection',[
'Passive protection begins when antibodies made by another individual reach the recipient.',
'Those antibodies act against their matching targets without requiring an initial antibody-production response by the recipient.'
],'''Maternal transfer includes antibodies crossing the placenta before birth and antibodies supplied in breast milk after birth. Antibodies in milk act particularly on the surfaces of the infant's gut and airways; not all swallowed antibodies pass into the baby's blood unchanged. Medically supplied antibodies can also give rapid temporary protection, for example after exposure to a dangerous infection. In each of these examples the recipient did not make the transferred antibodies.''',src=I)
c('3.8.3-10','PROC','Describe how an infant can acquire passive protection through breast milk.','First, antibodies made by the mother are supplied in her milk. They then help defend the infant, especially at mucosal surfaces.','1 mark: antibodies made by the mother pass into her milk and are taken in by the infant. 1 mark: then they act against pathogens, especially in the infant’s gut, so the infant is protected.')
e('3.8.3-11','Memory is not transferred',[
'Passive immunity does not produce the recipient’s memory cells merely by supplying antibodies.',
'When supplied antibodies decline, that passive protection declines unless another source replaces them.'
],'''Supplying antibodies does not stimulate the recipient's lymphocytes, so no memory cells are made. The infant may develop its own active responses to other exposures, but these are separate from the antibodies received in milk. Protection from passive antibody transfer is therefore not the same as the memory response produced by vaccination.

This explains why a baby breast-fed for several months still needs its own vaccinations: the milk antibodies protect for a while but leave no memory.''',src=I)
c('3.8.3-11','FEATURE','State whether receiving ready-made antibodies alone produces memory cells in the recipient.','It does not produce the recipient’s memory cells.','1 mark: one correct fact, that ready-made antibodies do not produce memory cells in the recipient, because the recipient’s lymphocytes have not been stimulated.')
e('3.8.3-12','Breast milk and infant protection',[
'Breast milk supplies maternal antibodies that can help protect a breast-fed infant against pathogens.',
'This passive protection is useful while the infant’s own immune responses are still developing.'
],'''Maternal antibody supply can reduce susceptibility, but feeding method alone does not decide whether an individual baby becomes ill. Hygiene, exposure, nutrition and other protective measures also matter. Breast milk does not guarantee absence of illness.

When a study compares groups of infants, first describe the data: which group has fewer infections and by how much. Then explain the possible mechanism: the antibodies in the milk act against pathogens in the gut. If the infants' own antibody production is similar in both groups, the difference is likely to come from the antibodies supplied by the mothers.''',src=I)
c('3.8.3-12','CHAIN','Explain why antibodies in breast milk are useful to a young infant.','Maternal antibodies help defend against pathogens. They provide temporary protection while the infant’s own immune responses develop.','1 mark: when an infant receives maternal antibodies in milk, the antibodies act against pathogens. 1 mark: so the infant has temporary protection while its own immune responses are still developing.')
c('3.8.3-12','APP','Explain a likely contribution of milk antibodies to this observation: two infant groups have similar exposure and nutrition, but the group receiving antibody-containing breast milk has fewer intestinal infections. Their own antibody production is similar.','Maternal antibodies in the milk can act against pathogens in the intestine. Because the infants’ own antibody production is similar, the difference is likely to come from the antibodies supplied by the mothers.','1 mark: the antibodies in this milk act against pathogens in the infants’ intestines. 1 mark: because the infants’ own antibody production is similar in both groups, the difference is likely to come from the antibodies supplied by the mothers (passive immunity).',demand=inference('Similar exposure, nutrition and infant antibody production; different external milk-antibody supply.','Explain the group difference through passive supply while ruling out increased own production.'))
e('3.8.3-13','Why passive protection fades',[
'Supplied antibodies are broken down over time, so their protective concentration falls unless replenished.',
'Passive transfer alone does not create a lasting recipient memory response to replace them rapidly.'
],'''Immediate availability and duration are different properties. Ready-made antibodies can act sooner than a first active response but give shorter protection. Repeated supply can maintain antibody availability, but it does not turn the mechanism into active immunity. If the recipient independently meets the antigen and makes its own memory cells, that additional response is active and should be identified separately.

Think of a graph of antibody concentration against time. After a dose of ready-made antibodies the concentration is high at once and then falls steadily as the antibodies are broken down. After vaccination the concentration rises more slowly but is kept up, and rises again quickly on later exposure because of memory cells.''',src=I)
c('3.8.3-13','PROC','Explain why passive immunity is short-term when no further antibodies are supplied.','Supplied antibodies are gradually broken down. Because no memory cells were generated by the transfer, that passive supply is not rapidly replaced by a memory response.','1 mark: the supplied antibodies are gradually broken down. 1 mark: then, because the transfer produced no memory cells, nothing replaces them, so protection fades.')
c('3.8.3-13','APP','Explain why protection from a dose of ready-made antibodies falls after several weeks, while a vaccinated comparison group responds rapidly to the same antigen later. Neither group receives more treatment.','The supplied antibodies are broken down without transfer-induced memory in the first group. Vaccination generated memory cells, allowing a rapid active response in the comparison group.','1 mark: the supplied antibodies are broken down and no memory cells were made, so protection in this first group falls. 1 mark: because vaccination produced memory cells, the vaccinated group can respond quickly when the antigen returns.',demand=inference('No repeat treatment; declining passive protection but rapid vaccinated response later.','Connect contrasting time courses to antibody decay versus immune memory.'))

# GAS EXCHANGE

e('3.9-01','Breathing and respiration',[
'Breathing is the movement of air into and out of the lungs.',
'Respiration consists of chemical reactions in cells that release energy from nutrient molecules.'
],'''Breathing ventilates the exchange surfaces, while respiration creates the cellular demand for oxygen during aerobic activity. Gas exchange is a third related process: oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse between the alveoli and the blood. A muscle cell can respire without directly moving air. The lungs do not make energy by breathing. The aerobic and anaerobic pathways are developed in 3.10.1 and 3.10.2; ventilation is a physical movement of air and not a chemical reaction.

The three terms fit together in order: breathing brings oxygen to the alveoli, gas exchange moves it into the blood, and respiration uses it in the cells to release energy. In an exam answer, use breathing only for the movement of air.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','39.1 Systems of Gas Exchange; 7.1 Energy in Living Systems','paraphrase'))
c('3.9-01','DIST','Distinguish breathing from respiration.','Breathing moves air into and out of lungs. Respiration is chemical energy release in cells from nutrients.','1 mark: breathing is the movement of air into and out of the lungs. 1 mark: whereas respiration is the chemical reactions in cells that release energy from nutrients.')
e('3.9-02','An effective exchange surface',[
'Alveoli provide a large total surface area and thin exchange surfaces for diffusion between air and blood.',
'A good blood supply and ventilation maintain oxygen and carbon dioxide concentration gradients across the exchange surface.'
],'''Numerous alveoli distribute a large area within the lungs. Thin alveolar and capillary walls give a short diffusion path. Ventilation replaces used alveolar air with fresh air, and blood flow brings blood needing oxygen while removing blood that has gained it. Both processes help preserve gradients: a large surface cannot exchange rapidly if no useful concentration difference remains.

Oxygen diffuses from alveolar air towards the blood when its concentration is higher in the air; carbon dioxide moves in the opposite direction when its concentration is higher in the arriving blood. The gases move by diffusion alone and are not pumped through the alveolar wall. A moist lining lets the gases dissolve before they diffuse across.

Adaptation questions are answered best as feature plus effect: for example, walls one cell thick give a short diffusion distance, and a rich capillary network keeps removing oxygen-rich blood so that the gradient is maintained.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','39.1 Systems of Gas Exchange: alveoli; 39.2 Gas Exchange across Respiratory Surfaces','paraphrase'))
c('3.9-02','FEATURE','List two features of human gas exchange surfaces that shorten diffusion distance or increase the available exchange area.','A thin surface. A large surface area.','1 mark: names one feature that shortens the diffusion distance, such as thin walls (one cell thick). 1 mark: names one feature that increases the area, such as the large number of alveoli giving a large surface area. A good blood supply or a moist lining is also accepted as a named feature.')
e('3.9-03','Intercostal muscles and ventilation',[
'External intercostal contraction raises the ribs during inspiration; the diaphragm contracts and flattens, increasing thoracic volume.',
'Quiet expiration largely follows relaxation and elastic recoil; internal intercostals help pull ribs down during forced expiration.'
],'''When thoracic volume increases, the pressure inside falls below the outside pressure and air moves into the lungs. Relaxation allows the chest to fall back, reducing the volume and moving air out. Forced expiration adds active muscle contraction, with the internal intercostal muscles pulling the ribs down; they do not need to contract strongly during every quiet breath. The diaphragm is a separate muscular sheet below the lungs, not one of the ribs.

Inspiration in order: external intercostals contract and the ribs move up and out, the diaphragm contracts and flattens, the volume of the chest increases, the pressure falls and air flows in. Expiration reverses these changes.''',src=G)
c('3.9-03','FEATURE','State the action of the external intercostal muscles on the ribs during inspiration.','They contract and raise the ribs (upwards and outwards).','1 mark: one correct fact, that the external intercostal muscles contract and raise the ribs upwards and outwards.')
e('3.9-04','Keeping the trachea open',[
'Cartilage supports the tracheal wall and helps prevent the airway collapsing during breathing.',
'Tracheal cartilage forms incomplete rings that allow flexibility while maintaining an open air passage.'
],'''During inspiration the pressure within the airways falls. A soft unsupported tube could narrow; cartilage resists this collapse. Cartilage is structural support, not the tissue that makes mucus or exchanges oxygen. The rings are C-shaped, and the gap at the back lets food pass down the oesophagus behind the trachea. Gas exchange happens mainly at the alveoli and not through the thick tracheal wall.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','39.1 Systems of Gas Exchange: trachea','paraphrase'))
c('3.9-04','FEATURE','State the function of cartilage in the trachea.','It supports the airway and prevents collapse during breathing.','1 mark: one correct function, that the cartilage supports the airway and keeps it open so it does not collapse.')
e('3.9-05','Inspired and expired air',[
'Expired air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than inspired air because of gas exchange with respiring tissues via the blood.',
'Expired air is generally warmer and contains more water vapour after passing over moist respiratory surfaces.'
],'''Typical classroom values are about 21% oxygen in inspired air and about 16% in mixed expired air; carbon dioxide rises from roughly 0.04% to around 4%. These values are approximate and vary with activity and sampling. Nitrogen changes little because it is not used in normal cellular respiration. Expired air still contains oxygen: the body does not remove it all in one breath, which is why mouth-to-mouth resuscitation can work.

Air breathed out first from the airways has not exchanged gases at the alveoli, so a mixed breath differs from a pure alveolar sample. When interpreting two samples of air, first identify the oxygen and carbon dioxide pattern, then check that the samples were collected in the same way. Temperature alone cannot identify the air if the collection conditions differ.

Worked example: sample X has 20.9% oxygen and 0.04% carbon dioxide; sample Y has 16.0% oxygen and 4.0% carbon dioxide. Y has 4.9 percentage points less oxygen and 3.96 points more carbon dioxide, so Y is expired air.''',src=('SRC-OSB2E','39.2 Gas Exchange across Respiratory Surfaces; 39.3 Breathing','paraphrase'))
c('3.9-05','PROC','Explain why expired air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than inspired air.','Oxygen first diffuses into blood for use in cellular respiration. Carbon dioxide from respiring tissues is then carried to the lungs and diffuses into the outgoing air.','1 mark: oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood and is used in respiration, so less oxygen is left in the air. 1 mark: then carbon dioxide made by respiring cells is carried to the lungs and diffuses into the alveolar air, so expired air contains more of it.')
c('3.9-05','APP','Explain which collected sample is likely expired air: sample X has 20.8% oxygen and 0.05% carbon dioxide; sample Y has 16.4% oxygen and 3.8% carbon dioxide. Samples were collected at the same time and analysed identically.','Y: it has less oxygen because oxygen entered blood. Its higher carbon dioxide fits addition from blood carrying respiratory waste.','1 mark: sample Y is expired air, because it has less oxygen (16.4% compared with 20.8%). 1 mark: this sample also has more carbon dioxide (3.8% compared with 0.05%), so both gas changes show that Y has been through the lungs.',demand=inference('Two numerical gas compositions with matched sampling.','Infer sample identity from two complementary gas changes.'))
e('3.9-06','Testing air for carbon dioxide',[
'Carbon dioxide turns limewater cloudy; the response can compare samples when equal air volumes pass through equal amounts of indicator.',
'Expired air normally produces a stronger or faster carbon-dioxide response than inspired air under matched testing conditions.'
],'''For a controlled written investigation, collect a known volume of room air and the same volume of expired air separately using clean suitable equipment under teacher supervision. Pass each sample through equal fresh limewater portions at the same delivery rate and temperature. Record the time to a predefined cloudiness or compare cloudiness after the same volume. Repeat with fresh indicator. Wear eye protection and prevent mouth contact or sucking liquid back through a tube; use separate collection and delivery equipment.

Hydrogencarbonate indicator offers an alternative: more carbon dioxide shifts the usual red or orange appearance towards yellow. Controls and starting colour must be matched. Cloudiness in limewater shows the presence of carbon dioxide; it does not show oxygen consumption. Unequal air volumes cannot give a fair comparison of concentration, because a larger volume would make the response stronger even at the same concentration.''',src=None)
c('3.9-06','SYNTH','Describe a safe investigation comparing carbon dioxide concentration in inspired and expired air using limewater.','Change the air sample: room air or separately collected expired air. Measure cloudiness after equal volumes pass through equal fresh limewater portions. Keep delivery rate, indicator volume and temperature the same. Use clean separate collection/delivery equipment and eye protection, preventing backflow towards the mouth. Repeat with fresh indicator and compare responses.','1 mark: the independent variable is changed: room air compared with expired air. 1 mark: the dependent variable is measured: the cloudiness of the limewater, or the time taken to turn cloudy. 1 mark: at least two variables are kept the same, such as the volume of air, the volume of limewater, the delivery rate and the temperature. 1 mark: the method is safe: separate clean tubes, eye protection and a set-up that stops limewater being sucked back towards the mouth. 1 mark: the test is repeated with fresh limewater, so the result is reliable.')
c('3.9-06','LIM','Suggest the problem if a learner bubbles 50 cm³ of room air but 250 cm³ of expired air through equal limewater portions, and an improvement.','A stronger response could be due to the larger air volume, not a greater carbon dioxide concentration. Pass equal volumes through fresh equal indicator portions.','1 mark: the problem is that the larger volume of expired air could cause more cloudiness even at the same carbon dioxide concentration, so the comparison is unfair. 1 mark: an improvement is to pass equal volumes of each sample through equal portions of fresh limewater.')
c('3.9-06','APP','Suggest a conclusion and one condition needed for this limewater result: equal air volumes produce more cloudiness with expired air than room air.','Expired air likely contains more carbon dioxide. This comparison requires the same starting limewater amount and concentration in both tests.','1 mark: expired air contains more carbon dioxide than room air. 1 mark: one condition, such as the same volume and concentration of fresh limewater in both tests, because otherwise this difference could explain the result.')
e('3.9-07','Breathing during exercise',[
'Physical activity raises muscle energy demand, commonly increasing oxygen use and carbon dioxide production.',
'Breathing usually becomes faster and deeper, increasing air movement to maintain exchange during greater demand.'
],'''Rate is breaths per minute; depth is the volume per breath. These can change independently, so rate alone is not a complete measure of ventilation. For illustration, 12 breaths/min at 0.5 dm³ per breath gives 6 dm³/min, whereas 20 breaths/min at 1.0 dm³ gives 20 dm³/min. The increase reflects both factors, not rate alone.

After activity stops, ventilation may stay above the resting level for a time as the body recovers. Deeper breathing does not mean that the alveoli become muscular pumps; the diaphragm and intercostal muscles do the work.

On a trace of breath volume against time, the number of peaks in one minute is the breathing rate, and the height of each peak shows the volume per breath. During exercise there are more peaks and they are taller, so more air is moved each minute.''',src=G)
c('3.9-07','CHAIN','Explain why breathing commonly becomes faster and deeper during moderate exercise.','Active muscles need more oxygen for energy release and produce more carbon dioxide. Faster, deeper breathing increases ventilation to support exchange.','1 mark: when muscles are more active, they respire faster, so they need more oxygen and produce more carbon dioxide. 1 mark: so the breathing becomes faster and deeper, increasing ventilation and gas exchange.')
c('3.9-07','APP','Fig. VIS-6116-3.9-07 is an illustrative trace of breath volume against time at rest and then during exercise. Explain how the trace shows that more air was moved per minute during exercise than at rest. Refer to the number of breaths and the size of each breath.','During exercise there are more peaks in each minute, so the breathing rate is higher, and each peak is taller, so the volume per breath is greater. More breaths of larger volume mean more air is moved per minute.','1 mark: the exercise part of this trace has more peaks per minute, so the breathing rate is higher. 1 mark: each peak is taller, because the volume per breath is greater; together these mean more air is moved per minute.',True,demand=inference('Illustrative respiratory-volume trace with resting and activity intervals.','Integrate trace frequency and volume amplitude to infer ventilation.'))
e('3.9-08','Investigating exercise and breathing',[
'Breathing rate can be measured by counting complete breaths over a fixed time; depth requires a volume-sensitive measure or a clearly identified estimate.',
'An exercise comparison must standardise activity and post-exercise timing, with recovery before repeated trials.'
],'''A safe school study can measure a rested volunteer before and after comfortable standardised activity. Count complete inspiration-expiration cycles for a defined interval. Use a suitable calibrated volume recorder under trained supervision to measure depth; clean equipment and individual mouthpieces are needed where applicable. Chest movement alone is an estimate of breathing depth, not a direct volume reading.

Keep duration, pace, posture and the interval before measurement constant. A delayed count underestimates the immediate response because recovery has already begun. Obtain consent, exclude participation where school health guidance advises it and stop if discomfort occurs. Repeat after full recovery. Comparisons between different people need care because fitness, age and body size also affect the response.

Worked example: at rest 15 breaths/min at 0.40 dm³ per breath gives 6.0 dm³/min. After activity, 25 breaths/min at 0.80 dm³ gives 20 dm³/min, an increase of 14 dm³/min.''',src=None)
c('3.9-08','SYNTH','Describe a safe investigation of moderate activity effects on breathing rate and depth.','Change rest versus a fixed comfortable stepping activity. Measure complete breaths/min and volume/breath with supervised calibrated breathing equipment. Take rested readings and readings at fixed times after activity. Keep participant, pace, activity duration and posture the same; use clean individual equipment and voluntary participation. Repeat after full recovery.','1 mark: the independent variable is changed: rest compared with a fixed comfortable activity. 1 mark: the dependent variables are measured: breaths per minute, and volume per breath using calibrated equipment. 1 mark: at least two variables are kept the same, such as the participant, pace, duration and timing. 1 mark: rested readings are taken first, then readings at fixed times after the activity. 1 mark: the test is repeated after full recovery and a mean calculated, so the result is reliable. Clean individual mouthpieces and voluntary participation are expected but not separately credited.')
c('3.9-08','LIM','Suggest a limitation of using chest movement alone as a measure of breath volume, and an improvement.','Chest displacement is not a direct calibrated air volume and varies with posture or movement. Use supervised calibrated volume-measuring equipment while keeping posture constant.','1 mark: the limitation is that chest movement is only an estimate, not a direct measure of the volume of air, and it varies with posture. 1 mark: an improvement is to use calibrated volume-measuring equipment under supervision.')
c('3.9-08','APP','Suggest two conclusions for one participant: at rest 14 breaths/min and 0.45 dm³/breath; after activity 24 breaths/min and 0.90 dm³/breath. Measurements used the same calibrated equipment.','Breathing became faster after activity. Breath depth also increased.','1 mark: the breathing rate of this participant increased, from 14 to 24 breaths/min. 1 mark: the volume per breath also increased, from 0.45 to 0.90 dm³, so more air was moved in each breath.')
for n,h,claims,x,p,a,g1,p2,a2,g2 in [
('09','Carbon monoxide',['Carbon monoxide combines strongly with haemoglobin, reducing its availability to carry oxygen.','Reduced oxygen delivery can limit aerobic energy release in tissues and add strain to the cardiovascular system.'],'''Carbon monoxide acts in the blood: it binds to haemoglobin much more strongly than oxygen does, so less oxygen can be carried. It does not coat the airways; that is the effect of tar. A person can move air normally while the blood carries too little oxygen, because ventilation and oxygen transport are different stages.

The result is that tissues, including the heart and the brain, receive less oxygen. In a pregnant smoker the baby also receives blood with less oxygen, which is one reason why smoking in pregnancy is harmful.''','Describe how carbon monoxide in tobacco smoke interferes with oxygen supply.','It binds strongly to haemoglobin. Less haemoglobin is available to transport oxygen.','1 mark: states that carbon monoxide binds strongly to haemoglobin. 1 mark: states the effect, that less haemoglobin is available to carry oxygen, so less oxygen is delivered to the tissues. Each mark is for a separate point.','Explain why carbon monoxide exposure can reduce muscle oxygen supply even when breathing rate is unchanged.','Haemoglobin is occupied by carbon monoxide. The blood therefore carries less oxygen despite continued ventilation.','1 mark: gives the reason that the haemoglobin is occupied by carbon monoxide. 1 mark: so the blood carries less oxygen even though ventilation continues, because oxygen transport and not breathing is affected.'),
('10','Nicotine',['Nicotine is an addictive component of tobacco smoke.','Nicotine can increase heart rate and constrict blood vessels, raising cardiovascular workload.'],'''Nicotine causes dependence, which makes stopping tobacco use difficult. Its other effects are on the circulation: it increases heart rate and constricts blood vessels, so the heart works against greater resistance. Its main link to the gas exchange system is addiction: dependence keeps the smoker breathing in the tar and carbon monoxide that damage the airways and reduce oxygen carriage.''','Describe two effects of nicotine relevant to tobacco use.','It promotes dependence. It can increase heart rate and constrict vessels.','1 mark: states that nicotine is addictive and promotes dependence. 1 mark: states one effect on the circulation, that it increases heart rate or constricts blood vessels. Each mark is for a separate point.','Explain why nicotine can increase strain on the cardiovascular system.','It can increase heart rate and narrow blood vessels. The heart must work harder to maintain circulation.','1 mark: gives the reason that nicotine increases heart rate and narrows blood vessels. 1 mark: so the heart must work harder to keep blood circulating, which increases the strain on the cardiovascular system.'),
('11','Tar and airway damage',['Tar in tobacco smoke contains cancer-causing substances and damages respiratory surfaces.','Tar-related airway injury impairs normal cleaning and contributes to mucus accumulation and chronic respiratory disease.'],'''Tar contains carcinogens, substances that increase the risk of cancer in the lungs and airways. It also damages the cilia that normally sweep mucus and trapped pathogens out of the airways. Without working cilia, mucus builds up, irritants and pathogens remain, and the smoker develops a persistent cough and frequent chest infections.

In chronic disease, damaged alveolar walls reduce the exchange area and the loss of elastic recoil makes expiration harder. These effects on the structure of the airways and alveoli are different from carbon monoxide reducing oxygen carriage in the blood. Different smoke components harm the same person by separate mechanisms.''','Describe two harmful effects of tar on the respiratory system.','It damages airway cleaning mechanisms. Its carcinogenic substances increase the risk of respiratory cancers.','1 mark: states that tar damages the cilia, so the airways are cleaned less effectively and mucus builds up. 1 mark: states that tar contains carcinogens that increase the risk of cancer in the respiratory system. Each mark is for a separate point.','Explain why airway damage associated with tar can lead to persistent mucus and more infections.','Damaged cilia move mucus out less effectively. Mucus and trapped pathogens remain in the airways.','1 mark: gives the reason that damaged cilia move mucus out less effectively. 1 mark: so mucus and trapped pathogens stay in the airways, because they are not swept out, which leads to more infections.')]:
 o='3.9-'+n;e(o,h,claims,x,src=None);c(o,'FEATURE',p,a,g1);c(o,'WHY',p2,a2,g2)
c('3.9-11','MISCON','A learner says that tar is the part of tobacco smoke that both reduces the oxygen the blood can carry and stops the cilia clearing mucus from the airways. Explain the mistake and name the smoke component responsible for each effect.','Tar does damage the cilia, so mucus is not cleared from the airways. It is carbon monoxide, not tar, that combines with haemoglobin and reduces the oxygen the blood carries.','1 mark: explains the mistake, that carbon monoxide instead of tar combines with haemoglobin and reduces the oxygen the blood carries. 1 mark: names tar as the component that damages the cilia, so mucus is not cleared.')
e('3.9-12','Smoking and COPD',[
'Tobacco smoking can cause chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, abbreviated COPD.',
'COPD involves persistent limitation of airflow, including changes associated with chronic bronchitis and emphysema.'
],'''Chronic bronchitis involves long-lasting airway inflammation and mucus problems. Emphysema damages the alveolar walls and elastic recoil, reducing the useful exchange area and making air harder to expel. COPD is not the same disease as lung cancer, though smoking increases the risk of both. Some people develop COPD from other exposures such as long-term dust or fumes, so smoking is a major risk factor but not the only cause.

A person with COPD becomes breathless on little effort, because less oxygen can cross the damaged exchange surface and the airways are narrowed. The damage cannot be reversed, although stopping smoking slows further decline.''',src=None)
c('3.9-12','FEATURE','State the chronic obstructive respiratory disease that tobacco smoking can cause.','Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).','1 mark: names the disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).')

summaries={
'3.7':'''| Route or process | Essential idea |
|---|---|
| Root | Outer epidermis and root hairs; cortex; central xylem with phloem between its arms |
| Stem | Vascular bundles in a ring; xylem inside each bundle, phloem outside |
| Water uptake | Osmosis into root cells when the soil-to-cell water-potential gradient favours entry |
| Xylem stream | Evaporation from leaves pulls connected water upwards |
| Transpiration | Evaporation at mesophyll surfaces, then vapour diffusion through stomata |
| Environment | Temperature, wind, humidity and stomatal opening change water loss under stated conditions |
| Wilting | Loss of cell turgor when water loss exceeds replacement |
| Translocation | Phloem carries sucrose and amino acids from sources to receiving regions |''',
'3.8.1':'''| Structure or process | Essential role |
|---|---|
| Atria and ventricles | Receive blood and pump it out respectively |
| Valves and septum | Limit backflow and separate right from left circulation |
| Arteries | Strong elastic walls carry blood away from the heart |
| Veins | Low-pressure return, wide lumens and valves |
| Capillaries | Thin exchange walls near body cells |
| Double circulation | Heart–lungs–heart and heart–body–heart |
| Coronary disease | Restricted oxygen delivery to heart muscle; risk is not certainty |
| Lymphatics | Return excess tissue fluid and support defence |''',
'3.8.2':'''| Blood component | Function or recognition |
|---|---|
| Red cells | Mature human cells lack nuclei; haemoglobin carries oxygen |
| White cells | Nucleated defence cells |
| Lymphocytes | Specific responses including antibody production |
| Phagocytes | Engulf and digest pathogens |
| Platelets | Help start clotting |
| Plasma | Liquid transport medium |
| Clot | Fibrinogen becomes fibrin; fibres trap cells |
| Tissue fluid | Exchange medium between capillary blood and cells |''',
'3.8.3':'''| Defence | Essential distinction |
|---|---|
| Mechanical | Natural structures such as intact skin restrict entry |
| Chemical | Stomach acid can destroy swallowed pathogens |
| Phagocytosis | Engulfing and digestion by a white cell |
| Antibodies | Bind matching antigens |
| Active immunity | Antibodies produced in the body; memory can remain |
| Passive immunity | Ready-made antibodies from another individual; short-term, no transferred memory |
| Vaccination | Stimulates active protection before later exposure |
| Breast milk | Supplies maternal antibodies while infant responses develop |''',
'3.9':'''| Idea | Essential distinction |
|---|---|
| Breathing | Air movement into and out of lungs |
| Respiration | Energy-releasing chemical reactions in cells |
| Exchange | Diffusion across a large, thin, ventilated surface with good blood supply |
| Inspiration | External intercostals raise ribs; diaphragm flattens |
| Expiration | Quiet recoil; internal intercostals help forced expiration |
| Expired air | Less oxygen, more carbon dioxide; generally warmer and moister |
| Activity | Usually increases breathing rate and depth |
| Carbon monoxide | Reduces haemoglobin oxygen carriage |
| Nicotine | Dependence and cardiovascular stimulation |
| Tar | Airway damage and carcinogens |'''
}
